window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["12"]={"https://theconversation.com/labor-slumps-to-new-lows-in-two-victorian-polls-296132":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"517ae165afd5f576da27facfca33e4438ac01e54d1909f7ec20009792427bda8","read":"https://theconversation.com/labor-slumps-to-new-lows-in-two-victorian-polls-296132","bytes":181453,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Labor slumps to new lows in two Victorian polls","by":[{"n":"Adrian Beaumont","r":"Election Analyst (Psephologist) at The Conversation; and Honorary Associate, School of Mathematics and Statistics, The University of Melbourne"}],"pub":"2026-11-10T05:15:26Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["With less than three weeks until the November 28 Victorian election, a DemosAU poll has Labor’s primary vote plunging seven points since September to just 18%, only one point ahead of the Greens. The Resolve poll isn’t as dramatic, but Labor’s 22% is still an equal record low."]},{"k":"p","s":["In both polls, the Coalition would easily win the election with 55–56% of the two-party vote against Labor. In all recent polls, the Coalition has gained to take a big lead. They are likely to win a majority of the 88 lower house seats in their own right. The graph below shows how bad the polls have become for Labor."]},{"k":"p","s":["If the DemosAU poll is accurate, Labor is facing a near-total wipeout, losing left-wing inner city seats to the Greens and other seats to the Coalition and One Nation. Liberal preference recommendations between Labor and the Greens could be important."]},{"k":"p","s":["I believe the recent poll movement is because people are now focusing on the state election, and they want Labor out. Labor has governed in Victoria for the last 12 years and 23 of the last 27."]},{"k":"p","s":["This article also includes a Victorian Pakenham seat poll and two more federal polls."]},{"k":"h","s":["DemosAU Victorian poll"]},{"k":"p","s":["A Victorian ",["DemosAU and PremierNational poll","https://www.heraldsun.com.au/news/victoria/state-election/coalition-surges-to-5644-lead-as-victorian-labor-faces-catastrophic-state-election-wipeout/news-story/cfd77a95d33cd90b152b633f1f4642cb"]," for The Herald Sun, conducted October 30 to November 5 from a sample of 1,562, gave the Coalition 33% of the primary vote (up three since the ",["mid-September DemosAU poll","https://demosau.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/DemosAU-Report-Victoria-Poll-September-2026.pdf"],"), One Nation 21% (up one), Labor 18% (down seven), the Greens 17% (up three) and all Others 11% (steady)."]},{"k":"p","s":["After preferences, the Coalition led Labor by 56–44, a four-point gain for the Coalition. On these primary votes, one of Labor or the Greens would make the final two in most seats, surpassing One Nation on the other left party’s preferences."]},{"k":"p","s":["Labor premier Ben Carroll’s net positive score slumped 16 points -30 (47% negative, 17% positive). Liberal leader Jess Wilson’s was up five points to +13 (38% positive, 25% negative). One Nation state leader Warren Pickering’s was down two points to -20 (43% negative, 23% positive). Greens leader Ellen Sandell was at -20 (38% negative, 18% positive)."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a three-way preferred premier question, Wilson had 36%, Carroll 28% and Pickering 19%. Wilson led Carroll by 48–30 head to head. Some figures are from ",["The Poll Bludger","https://www.pollbludger.net/2026/11/09/demosau-labor-18-coalition-33-one-nation-21-in-victoria/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Victorian Resolve poll"]},{"k":"p","s":["A Victorian ",["Resolve poll","https://www.theage.com.au/politics/victoria/half-of-victorians-open-to-a-one-nation-coalition-as-carroll-faces-labor-wipeout-20261109-p61cjv.html"]," for The Age, conducted November 2–7 from a sample of 1,000, gave the Coalition 30% of the primary vote (up two since the early October Resolve poll), One Nation 23% (steady), Labor 22% (down three), the Greens 13% (down two), independents 6% (up two) and others 7% (up two). This is tied with January 2025 for Labor’s worst primary vote in this poll."]},{"k":"p","s":["No two-party estimate was provided, but analyst ",["Kevin Bonham estimated a Coalition","https://x.com/kevinbonham/status/2119683731883749870"]," lead over Labor by about 55–45, a three-point gain for the Coalition."]},{"k":"p","s":["Carroll’s net likeability was down five points to -16, his worst since he replaced Jacinta Allan as premier in late July. Wilson’s has improved in each poll since July and is now at a record high of +24 (up one since October and up 11 since July). Pickering’s has also improved in every poll since August and is now at +9 (up one since October and up nine since August)."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a three-way preferred premier question, Wilson had 35% (up six), Carroll 21% (down one) and Pickering 15% (up one)."]},{"k":"p","s":["By 48–38, respondents were comfortable with the Coalition governing in partnership with One Nation, but by 50–38 they were not comfortable with Labor governing with the Greens."]},{"k":"h","s":["Victorian Pakenham seat poll"]},{"k":"p","s":["Pickering is One Nation’s candidate for ",["Pakenham","https://www.abc.net.au/news/elections/vic/2026/guide/pake"],", which Labor won by 50.4–49.6 against the Liberals at the 2022 election. A ",["TKW Research/DemosAU","https://www.heraldsun.com.au/news/victoria/state-election/one-nation-poised-to-win-first-victorian-lower-house-seat-amid-pakenham-poll-surge/news-story/0ee84fdba88eed1dda2f35b5f3c60135"]," poll for The Herald Sun, conducted November 4–8 from a small sample of 484, gave Pickering 40% of the primary vote, the Liberals 24% and Labor 14% with no other candidates listed."]},{"k":"p","s":["Despite Pickering’s 16-point lead on primary votes, he’s tied after preferences at 50–50 with the Liberals, indicating a strong flow of Labor and other preferences to the Liberals. Seat polls are unreliable."]},{"k":"h","s":["Candidate nominations for the election"]},{"k":"p","s":["Candidate nominations for the Victorian election closed on Monday. ABC election analyst ",["Antony Green said there are a total","https://antonygreen.com.au/vic2026-candidate-numbers-at-close-of-nominations/"]," of 734 lower house candidates for the 88 seats, an average of 8.3 per seat. That’s down slightly from a record 740 candidates in 2022."]},{"k":"p","s":["Labor, the Coalition, One Nation, the Greens and the Victorian Socialists are contesting all 88 seats and Family First 71, with 91 independents."]},{"k":"p","s":["The upper house has eight regions that each return five members for a total of 40 seats. The scrapping of group ticket voting has resulted in the number of upper house candidates plunging from a record 454 in 2022 to 303, the lowest since 2010. There are an average of 12.4 groups per region, down from a record 22.2 in 2022."]},{"k":"h","s":["Federal Spectre poll"]},{"k":"p","s":["A national ",["Spectre Strategy poll","https://static1.squarespace.com/static/67cd0be72bb18b3c88b9af04/t/6af0f31063b80c7df5dd8a75/1794175760382/Political+Update+November+26-+Spectre+Strategy.pdf"],", conducted October 28 to November 3 from a sample of 2,093, gave One Nation 28% of the primary vote (up two since the ",["mid-July Spectre poll","https://static1.squarespace.com/static/67cd0be72bb18b3c88b9af04/t/6a66a724a5b0f842d4f3f3b2/1785112356745/Political+Update+July+26-+Spectre+Strategy.pdf"],"), Labor 25% (down three), the Coalition 24% (up one), the Greens 13% (up one) and all Others 10% (down one)."]},{"k":"p","s":["By respondent preferences, Labor led One Nation by 52–48, a two-point gain for One Nation. The Coalition led Labor by 53–47, a four-point gain for the Coalition."]},{"k":"p","s":["Anthony Albanese’s net favourability was down 14 points to a record low of -25 (54% unfavourable, 29% favourable). Angus Taylor’s was up five points to -2 (29% unfavourable, 27% favourable). Pauline Hanson’s was down three points to -4 (45% unfavourable, 41% favourable)."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a three-way preferred PM question, Albanese had 34%, Hanson 30% and Taylor 22%."]},{"k":"h","s":["Morgan poll"]},{"k":"p","s":["A national ",["Morgan poll","https://www.roymorgan.com/findings/10377-federal-voting-intention-november-9-2026"],", conducted October 26 to November 8 from a sample of 2,574, gave Labor 25.5% of the primary vote (down 0.5 since the mid-October Morgan poll), One Nation 24.5% (up two), the Coalition 22% (down 1.5), the Greens 15% (down 0.5) and all Others 13% (up 0.5)."]},{"k":"p","s":["By respondent preferences, Labor led One Nation by 54–46, a one-point gain for One Nation. Labor led the Coalition by 51–49, a 1.5-point gain for the Coalition. By 2025 election preference flows, Labor led the Coalition by 52.5–47.5, a 0.5-point gain for the Coalition."]},{"k":"p","s":["Morgan’s polls are taken over a fortnight. Weekly breakdowns had Labor leading the Coalition by 51.5–48.5 in late October, but only by 50.5–49.5 in early November."]},{"k":"h","s":["Essential poll: additional questions on Melbourne Cup"]},{"k":"p","s":["Additional questions on the Melbourne Cup from the ",["late October Essential poll","https://essentialreport.com.au/reports/28-october-2026"]," were released on Cup Day (November 3). On interest level, 68% (up 13) said they had low or no interest, 23% (down three) moderate interest and 9% (down nine) high interest."]},{"k":"p","s":["On betting, 46% (up six and up 11 since 2024) said they’re not interested in the Cup and won’t place a bet, 25% (up one) said they rarely bet on horse races, but would watch the Cup and place a bet, 17% (down one) said they would watch the Cup but not bet and 12% (down six) said they regularly bet on horse races."]}],"chars":6919},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/democratic-republic-of-the-congo":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"8762ac8dee605ed25f0c0604bf34d6db2269fc3d2c3cbad1180995963bf10568","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/democratic-republic-of-the-congo","bytes":104957,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Democratic Republic of the Congo travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-05T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-10-08T13:20:38+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel insurance could be invalidated if you travel against advice from the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas where FCDO advises against travel"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Areas in Kinshasa Province"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["The districts of N’djili and Kimbanseke in Kinshasa city south of the main access road to N’djili airport, in Nsele commune"]},{"k":"li","s":["The N1 road in Kinshasa Province, between and including Menkao to the west, Kenge to the east, the border of Mai-Ndombe province to the north, and 10km to the south"]},{"k":"hs","s":["DRC-Central African Republic border"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to within 50km of the border with the Central African Republic."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Eastern DRC"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to the provinces of:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Haut-Uélé and Ituri, including the entire DRC-South Sudan border"]},{"k":"li","s":["North Kivu"]},{"k":"li","s":["South Kivu"]},{"k":"li","s":["Maniema"]},{"k":"li","s":["Tanganyika"]},{"k":"li","s":["Haut-Lomami"]},{"k":"hs","s":["South-East DRC"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to Upemba National Park. If you choose to travel to Upemba National Park against FCDO advise, notify the park authorities about your travel plans."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Territory in Mai-Ndombe Province"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to the Kwamouth territory of Mai-Ndombe Province. This is between, and including, the towns of Kwamouth, Bandundu and the southern border of Mai-Ndombe province."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Kisangani airport"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to Bangoka International Airport, Kisangani due to multiple drone attacks in the area of Kisangani airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["See details about ",["why FCDO advises against travel","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/democratic-republic-of-the-congo/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Security situation in DRC"]},{"k":"hs","s":["North and South Kivu"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are in North or South Kivu and judge it safe to do so, and if routes are available, you should leave."]},{"k":"p","s":["M23 rebels and Rwanda Defence Forces (RDF) have captured the cities of Goma and Bukavu and the surrounding areas in North and South Kivu. M23 rebels and RDF captured the city of Uvira in December 2025, and then withdrew from the city in January 2026, though clashes continue in the surrounding areas. The situation remains highly unstable and unpredictable. Routes to depart Uvira, Goma and Bukavu are limited and may change at short notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["The border crossings between Rwanda and the DRC at Gisenyi-Goma and Ruzizi-Bukavu could close at short notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["Goma and Bukavu airports have been attacked and commercial flights are no longer operating from the airports."]},{"k":"p","s":["Support from the UK government is severely limited outside Kinshasa. You should not assume that FCDO will be able to provide assistance to leave the country in the event of serious unrest or crisis."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tshopo Province"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 23 May, there were multiple drone attacks in the area of Kisangani airport. These incidents have led to disruption to flights. The security situation remains unpredictable, and further disruption is possible."]},{"k":"h","s":["Ebola"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 17 May, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared the Ebola (Bundibugyo virus disease) outbreak in DRC a ",["Public Health Emergency of International Concern","https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern"],". The outbreak is highly active, evolving and geographically widespread."]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel to DRC read ",["Ebola Disease in DRC","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/news/909/ebola-disease-in-drc-and-uganda"]," and ",["general information on Ebola and similar diseases","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/68/viral-haemorrhagic-fever"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are in DRC read ",["how to reduce your risk from Ebola","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/democratic-republic-of-the-congo/health"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some countries have additional health screening requirements or entry restrictions for travellers arriving from, or who have recently visited the DRC. ",["Check entry requirements","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/"]," for any destination or transit country before travelling."]},{"k":"h","s":["Help and support in DRC"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read FCDO advice on how to deal with a crisis overseas."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Getting help in DRC"]},{"k":"p","s":["For help in an emergency from the British government, telephone: +243815566200"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["‘Getting help’","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/democratic-republic-of-the-congo/getting-help"]," for more information on other ways to get help in DRC."]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact FCDO in London"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/democratic-republic-of-the-congo/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC) set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["DRC Embassy in the UK","https://www.ambardc.london/c/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive in DRC."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a visa to visit DRC."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["Apply for a visa through the ",["DRC Embassy in the UK","https://forms.ambardc.london/visa/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Allow at least 2 to 3 weeks for application processing."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some types of visa applications are referred to Kinshasa for a decision. In these cases, if you submit a letter of invitation from the person or organisation you’re visiting, stamped by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs in Kinshasa, you may speed up the process."]},{"k":"p","s":["Consider getting a long-term visa if you are planning to stay in DRC for a considerable period. When you apply for or renew a long-term visa, DRC authorities need a ‘consular certificate’ as part of the application. The British Embassy in DRC can issue a consular certificate for you."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Checks at border control"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have a Rwandan or Ugandan immigration stamp in your passport you may have some difficulties on arrival, but it should not prevent you from entering DRC."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re crossing regularly between Rwanda and DRC you may have immigration difficulties if you have not regularised your residency status."]},{"k":"h","s":["Ebola"]},{"k":"p","s":["You may encounter health screening measures, such as being required to fill in a passenger locator form on arrival and departure and temperature checks, including at airports."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some countries have heightened health screening for travellers from DRC. ",["Check entry requirements","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/"]," for the country you’re travelling to or transiting."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Rwanda","https://x.com/RwandaHealth/status/2057790371657101712?s=20"]," and ",["Uganda","https://x.com/MinofHealthUG/status/2057471796497035293"]," have announced restrictions which impact travellers to DRC."]},{"k":"p","s":["The UK’s Returning Workers Scheme has been activated, due to the outbreak of Ebola disease. Returning health and humanitarian workers should check the latest at ",["UK’s Returning Workers Scheme","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/ebola-returning-workers-scheme"]," before returning to the UK."]},{"k":"h","s":["Departure tax"]},{"k":"p","s":["You need to pay a departure tax of 55 US dollars on international flights and 10 US dollars on domestic flights. You pay these fees when you check in for your departure. Get an official receipt and a copy for each fee. Hand the originals on request to immigration and at boarding and keep the copies."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter DRC, you must have certificates to prove you’ve had:"]},{"k":"li","s":["a yellow fever vaccination"]},{"k":"li","s":["a polio vaccine, in some circumstances"]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s DRC guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/63/democratic-republic-of-the-congo#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You may need a yellow fever certificate for onward travel to some other countries, due to the risk of yellow fever in DRC."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of DRC. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"p","s":["You may have difficulties when entering DRC if you are travelling with:"]},{"k":"li","s":["satellite phones"]},{"k":"li","s":["GPS receivers"]},{"k":"li","s":["military clothing"]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC)","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/democratic-republic-of-the-congo/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in DRC"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorists are very likely to try to carry out attacks in DRC."]},{"k":"p","s":["ADF, a militant group with links to Daesh, carry out regular attacks in North Kivu and Ituri provinces, eastern DRC. The majority of its attacks target villages in rural areas, but it has attacked military targets – primarily Ugandan and Congolese armed forces personnel. Attacks have also targeted bars, places of worship, markets and vehicle convoys."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate including in places visited by foreign nationals. Stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protests and demonstrations"]},{"k":"p","s":["A range of military and peacekeeping forces are deployed in eastern DRC. Tensions about the presence of these forces and the role of international actors can cause protests in Goma and Kinshasa. There have been large protests against the UN peacekeeping mission known as the United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission (MONUSCO). In February 2024 there were protests outside the British Embassy and other western embassies. Similar protests cannot be ruled out in future. These could be directed at foreign nationals or target UN vehicles and premises."]},{"k":"p","s":["On 28 January 2025, there were large and violent protests outside multiple embassies in Kinshasa, in response to the fighting in eastern DRC. This resulted in an increased security presence and there is an ongoing risk of sporadic protests, which can involve gun fire."]},{"k":"p","s":["Public gatherings and demonstrations can occur with little or no notice and can quickly turn violent."]},{"k":"p","s":["Protests may result in:"]},{"k":"li","s":["the closure of shops and public services"]},{"k":"li","s":["restrictions on movement"]},{"k":"p","s":["In the event of serious unrest:"]},{"k":"li","s":["commercial flights may be suspended"]},{"k":"li","s":["roads may be blocked"]},{"k":"li","s":["international borders may be closed at short notice, particularly those with Rwanda, at Gisenyi/Goma and Cyangugu/Bukavu"]},{"k":"li","s":["internet connections and mobile phone networks may have reduced services or be cut off"]},{"k":"li","s":["military and police presence in Kinshasa and other major cities may increase - this may include stop-and-search checkpoints, especially after dark"]},{"k":"p","s":["To minimise your personal risk:"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow local media for news of any planned protests"]},{"k":"li","s":["leave quickly and do not attempt to watch or photograph any demonstration or disturbance"]},{"k":"li","s":["consider making contingency plans"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep a stock of essential supplies"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep your travel documents up-to-date, including appropriate visas"]},{"k":"li","s":["be prepared to move or go into curfew at short notice if told to by the authorities"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["how to prepare for a crisis overseas","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"]]},{"k":"p","s":["Stay away from areas where protests are planned, particularly in Kinshasa, Goma and other large cities. This may include:"]},{"k":"li","s":["N’Djili airport, Kinshasa"]},{"k":"li","s":["Churches and other religious buildings and institutions"]},{"k":"li","s":["large sporting stadiums or music venues"]},{"k":"li","s":["universities"]},{"k":"li","s":["political party headquarters"]},{"k":"li","s":["foreign embassies"]},{"k":"li","s":["government and parliament buildings"]},{"k":"li","s":["offices of the Electoral Commission"]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not assume FCDO will be able to help you leave the country in the event of serious unrest."]},{"k":"h","s":["Traveling around DRC"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is widespread poverty, social exclusion and health risks in many parts of the DRC. You may be at greater risk of being robbed or attacked in rural areas. Transport and accommodation are extremely limited outside Kinshasa and the major towns, so make travel arrangements in advance. Travel in the company of experienced people or organisations."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be respectful of local customs and land rights, including the necessary permissions for activities. Recent incidents have occurred with local communities when foreign nationals have wild camped without permission."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Street crime and scamming"]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign nationals are at particular risk of street robbery in Kinshasa, Lubumbashi and other urban centres, especially near hotels and supermarkets in the centre of town. Organised gangs lure people into traps through techniques including the use of children, promising cut-price gold and diamonds, or posing as police or security personnel. Robberies by gangs of street children are common and can be aggressive."]},{"k":"p","s":["In Kinshasa, robberies can be carried out by people posing as taxi drivers or taxi passengers, and are often accompanied by a threat of violence."]},{"k":"p","s":["In North and South Kivu, reports of criminal activity have increased, particularly those targeting the international community. There have been many reports of robberies and banditry in Goma, especially after dark."]},{"k":"p","s":["To reduce your personal risk:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid walking in the streets alone at any time, especially at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not display valuables and cash"]},{"k":"li","s":["use a hotel safe if possible and keep copies of documents, including your passport, separately"]},{"k":"li","s":["use transport organised by a hotel or tour operator"]},{"k":"li","s":["if you must take a taxi, use a privately booked one and do not hail taxis in the street"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Criminal kidnap"]},{"k":"p","s":["Kidnapping is a risk in parts of Kinshasa, including the Limete area."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been several recent incidents where criminals pretending to be policemen or security personnel have captured foreign nationals and then demanded payment for their release."]},{"k":"p","s":["Criminals may also carry out ‘express kidnaps’, where they capture victims, take them to ATMs to withdraw money, and then release them."]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign nationals can be perceived as being wealthier than locals and may be at particular risk."]},{"k":"p","s":["To reduce risk:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid walking in the streets alone at any time, especially at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["Do not display valuables and cash"]},{"k":"li","s":["If you must use a taxi, use a privately booked one"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not hail taxis in the street."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Vehicle crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Carjackings are rare, but lock vehicle doors and keep windows closed when driving. Watch out for armed gangs who may target your car or try to enter it to rob you. Do not drive off the main roads or park in unsupervised areas."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Justice process"]},{"k":"p","s":["The DRC authorities rarely meet their international obligations to notify embassies when foreign nationals have been detained. Even if requested, adequate consular access is not always granted. Keep in close touch with family or friends and ask them to notify the ",["British Embassy in Kinshasa","https://www.gov.uk/world/organisations/british-embassy-kinshasa"]," immediately if you are arrested in DRC."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Using cameras in secure areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["Taking photographs of some government and military buildings is illegal, particularly borders or military installations. You risk being arrested and detained. Journalists should not conduct interviews or film without the necessary local permits."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity is not illegal in DRC but is frowned upon by many. There is no legal recognition of same-sex couples or same-sex marriage, and there are no specific anti-discrimination laws protecting LBGT+ people. Members of the LGBT+ community are known to be harassed. Individuals may be charged under DRC’s Penal Code for ‘crimes against family life’."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Adopting children from DRC"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against adopting from DRC. The UK does not recognise adoptions made in DRC as DRC is not a signatory to the Hague Convention on Protection of Children and Co-operation in Respect of Intercountry Adoption."]},{"k":"p","s":["The DRC government has not issued exit permits for children adopted by foreign nationals in DRC since 2013 and many are still unable to leave the country. It is illegal for same-sex or trans-sexual couples to adopt Congolese children."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Camouflage clothing"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you wear any military-style clothing you could be misidentified as military personnel and be vulnerable to attack or detention."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Money"]},{"k":"p","s":["Credit cards are only accepted in a limited number of places in Kinshasa and other cities. US dollars are widely used alongside the local currency and a number of ATMs dispense US dollars in Kinshasa. Notes should be in good condition and issued after 2001. Elsewhere in DRC, cash is needed for transactions."]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure you have access to money, or have arrangements in place for it to be sent to you in an emergency, before leaving the UK."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Democratic Republic of Congo, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],". You’ll need a ",["1968 International Driving Permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," to drive in DRC. You will not be able to buy an IDP outside the UK, so get one before you travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["Car hire is possible in Kinshasa although self-drive options are limited. Most car hire companies will only let you hire a car with a driver."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Driving standards are well below those in the UK and accidents are very common. Roads in the DRC are poorly maintained, largely unpaved and often not drivable with a standard vehicle, especially during the rainy season from September to May. Inside the cities, including Kinshasa, main roads are often paved but they can be narrow with cars parked on either side. Consider the technical capability of your vehicle and be confident in your ability to operate it. Travel after dark brings increased risk."]},{"k":"p","s":["Public buses and taxis are often not well-maintained, and there is a risk of assault."]},{"k":"p","s":["Security forces operate roadblocks, particularly after dark. If you are asked to produce documents for inspection at a checkpoint, stay inside your vehicle and show them through closed windows."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Air travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["UK Air Safety List (ASL)","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/uk-air-safety-list#democratic-republic-of-congo-drc"]," lists all known airlines in DRC that do not meet international safety standards and are banned from operating commercial air services to, from, and within the UK. Check the UK Air Safety List when considering which airlines to fly with. The list is maintained by the Department for Transport, based on advice from the ",["UK Civil Aviation Authority","https://www.caa.co.uk/Commercial-Industry/Airlines/Licensing/Requirements-and-guidance/Third-Country-Operator-Certificates"],". FCDO advises against all travel by air into and out of Goma airport. ",["See why FCDO advises against travel","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/democratic-republic-of-the-congo/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Rail travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["The railways are in a poor state and you should be aware of the increased risk of travel by train. A refurbished route opened between Kinshasa and Matadi in 2015, although services are infrequent and unreliable."]},{"k":"hs","s":["River travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["The ferry service between Kinshasa and Brazzaville stops running in the late afternoon and there is no service on Sundays."]},{"k":"p","s":["Boats and ferries on the rivers and lakes are poorly maintained and often overloaded. As a result of the low safety standards, high river traffic levels, strong currents, shifting sandbanks and poor maintenance there have been many accidents."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to ",["prepare for and respond to extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Flooding"]},{"k":"p","s":["During the wet season (approximately September to May), heavy rainfall often causes severe flooding in parts of DRC. Check for news of flooding before travelling."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Earthquakes are a risk in DRC, but they are infrequent. The US Federal Emergency Management Agency website has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after an earthquake","https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Volcanic eruptions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Mount Nyiragongo volcano, close to the city of Goma and near the border with Rwanda, erupted in 2002 and 2021, resulting in loss of lives and damage to infrastructure in and around Goma. Further volcanic activity is possible at any time."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of the Democratic Republic of Congo. It only covers regions where the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/democratic-republic-of-the-congo"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/democratic-republic-of-the-congo/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Eastern DRC"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to the provinces of:"]},{"k":"li","s":["North Kivu, including the city of Goma"]},{"k":"li","s":["South Kivu, including the city of Bukavu"]},{"k":"li","s":["Haut-Uélé and Ituri, including the entire DRC-South Sudan border"]},{"k":"li","s":["Maniema"]},{"k":"li","s":["Tanganyika"]},{"k":"li","s":["Haut Lomami"]},{"k":"p","s":["The security situation in eastern DRC remains unstable. Armed groups are active and intercommunal violence can affect the political, security and humanitarian situation. Large numbers of civilians remain displaced as a result of the conflict. Acts of violence, including killing, rape and looting continue against civilians."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are continued reports of towns in eastern DRC being attacked by or falling under temporary control of armed groups. There are continued reports of attacks and kidnappings, including against staff from NGOs and international organisations. Be prepared to leave at short notice via available routes. If you are in an area against FCDO advice, you should be vigilant at all times and keep your security situation under constant review."]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign nationals travelling in eastern DRC have been left vulnerable, without escorted transport. The risk of kidnap or injury remains high."]},{"k":"p","s":["While British government staff do visit areas of eastern DRC, the British Embassy’s ability to offer support is severely limited."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Peacekeeping forces"]},{"k":"p","s":["A United Nations peacekeeping presence is deployed in eastern DRC. The Congolese army is carrying out operations against foreign and domestic armed groups operating in North and South Kivu and Ituri provinces. Foreign, regional forces are also present in eastern DRC."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Goma and Bukavu"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to Goma in North Kivu and Bukavu in South Kivu in eastern DRC. The situation in eastern DRC is unpredictable, and there is a high risk of ongoing instability, particularly in Goma and Bukavu."]},{"k":"p","s":["The armed group M23 and Rwanda Defence Forces (RDF) have captured and taken control of Goma. Fighting in Goma and the surrounding areas has intensified. Goma airport is currently not operational and is under the control of RDF and M23."]},{"k":"p","s":["On 16 February, RDF and M23 forces captured and took control of Bukavu, the main city in South Kivu. This follows advances south from Goma. On Friday 14 February, M23 and RDF forces captured Kavuma airport, approximately 30km north of the city. Commercial flights are not operating from the airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Ruzizi border crossing in Bukavu is under RDF and M23 control and appears open, but the situation remain highly unpredictable and may change at short notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is an ongoing risk of protests in the east, particularly in Goma, North Kivu province. These could be directed at foreign nationals."]},{"k":"p","s":["A ‘state of siege’ is in place in the provinces of Ituri and North Kivu. Military administrations with extended security powers have replaced the civilian administrations. On 20 February 2024, the Governor of North Kivu banned vehicles from driving in the province with tinted windows (this includes the city of Goma)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The situation in Goma, Bukavu, and the surrounding areas remains uncertain, unstable and can change quickly. British nationals should be prepared to leave at short notice and have adequate supplies of food, water, medicine etc in case of the need to lock down."]},{"k":"h","s":["Tshopo Province"]},{"k":"p","s":["The security situation in and around Bangoka International Airport, Kisangani, remains unpredictable following recent drone attacks against DRC military assets at the airport. These attacks have been claimed by the M23. Commercial flights are operating as usual, but availability can change at short notice. These border crossings are controlled by RDF and M23."]},{"k":"h","s":["Border crossings with Rwanda"]},{"k":"p","s":["The border crossings between Rwanda and the DRC at Gisenyi/Goma and Cyangugu/Bukavu may close at short notice. These border crossings are controlled by RDF and M23."]},{"k":"p","s":["Following the Ebola outbreak, transit from the Democratic Republic of the Congo through Rwanda is only permitted in exceptional circumstances related to humanitarian missions and essential travel, and is subject to health risk assessment, destination-country, transport operator, and public health requirements, and approval by the Rwandan authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Territory in Mai-Ndombe Province"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to the Kwamouth territory of Mai-Ndombe Province. This is between, and including, the towns of Kwamouth, Bandundu and the southern border of Mai-Ndombe Province."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is ongoing intercommunal violence between ethnic groups over land. The government has deployed a military force to the area. We are not aware of any direct threat against the international community or British nationals. Be aware of the increased violence and potential threat."]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas in Kinshasa Province"]},{"k":"p","s":["Due to the risk of violent crime, FCDO advises against all but essential travel in the districts of:"]},{"k":"li","s":["N’djili and Kimbanseke in Kinshasa city, south of the main access road to N’djili airport, in Nsele commune"]},{"k":"li","s":["Kasa Vubu in Kinshasa City, bordered by the N1 Triumphal Road (Stadium), Barumbu District, Lingwala District and the south of the botanical garden"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the N1 road in Kinshasa Province, between and including Menkao to the west, Kenge to the east, the border of Mai-Ndombe province to the north, and 10km to the south. There has been fighting between local militia and Congolese Armed Forces on this section of the N1 road. There are checkpoints on the road and reports of access being blocked. The authorities advise to travel during daylight hours."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is no general emergency medical number in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC). If you have a medical emergency which requires an ambulance, contact a hospital to make this request."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for DRC","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/63/democratic-republic-of-the-congo#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in DRC","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/63/democratic-republic-of-the-congo"]," including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["ebola"]},{"k":"li","s":["malaria"]},{"k":"li","s":["cholera"]},{"k":"li","s":["yellow fever"]},{"k":"li","s":["mpox"]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of DRC. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Ebola"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are frequent outbreaks of serious infectious diseases in DRC. On 17 May, the World Health Organization (WHO) declared the Ebola (Bundibugyo virus disease) outbreak in DRC a ",["Public Health Emergency of International Concern","https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern"],". The outbreak is active, evolving and geographically widespread."]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel to DRC read ",["Ebola Disease in DRC","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/news/909/ebola-disease-in-drc-and-uganda"]," and ",["general information on Ebola and similar diseases","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/68/viral-haemorrhagic-fever"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some countries have additional health screening requirements or entry restrictions for travellers arriving from, or who have recently visited the DRC. ",["Check entry requirements","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/"]," for any destination or transit country before travelling."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Health Security Agency has ",["Ebola information for humanitarian or healthcare workers","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ebola-virus-disease-information-for-workers-in-at-risk-countries"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ebola spreads through direct contact with the blood, body fluids or tissues of an infected person, or through contact with contaminated materials. To reduce your risk:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid contact with anyone who is unwell or showing symptoms of Ebola"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid contact with blood, body fluids and human remains"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid handling bushmeat or contact with bats and other wild animals"]},{"k":"li","s":["wash your hands regularly and follow local public health advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you develop symptoms such as fever, severe headache, muscle pain, diarrhoea, vomiting or unexplained bleeding while in DRC, seek medical advice immediately and avoid contact with others. Symptoms can develop up to 21 days after exposure."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in DRC"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in DRC","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/medical-practitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical care is extremely limited. The Centre Prive d’Urgence (CPU) clinic in Kinshasa is able to cope with basic health problems and to stabilise a patient after most serious accidents. Outside Kinshasa, Western-standard medical facilities are almost non-existent. Medical evacuation to South Africa (or elsewhere) could be necessary. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you take medication regularly, make sure you bring enough for your time in the DRC, and that it is in properly marked packaging."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in DRC"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is no general emergency medical number in Kinshasa or elsewhere in the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC)."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some major cities have emergency services, but their level of assistance is limited."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have a medical emergency which requires an ambulance, contact a hospital to make this request. If you are in Kinshasa, you can contact the fire service on 118, and the police on 112."]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Congo%2C%20Democratic%20Republic"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Congo%2C%20Democratic%20Republic"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Congo%2C%20Democratic%20Republic"]]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in DRC","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/bereavement-information-pack-democratic-republic-of-congo"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in DRC","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/democratic-republic-of-congo-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/democratic-republic-of-the-congo/british-embassy-kinshasa"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/democratic-republic-of-the-congo/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]}],"chars":30829,"change":"New information and guidance about the ongoing Ebola outbreak in DRC (see 'Warnings and insurance' and 'Health' pages)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ecuador":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"d0b297bb51142d293a008984e0657c81079b050b99144a3903f967d9c0e7767c","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/ecuador","bytes":124122,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Ecuador travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-15T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-11-01T19:30:00+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel insurance could be invalidated if you travel against advice from the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas where FCDO advises against all but essential travel"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Coastal Region"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the Coastal Region provinces of:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Esmeraldas"]},{"k":"li","s":["Manabí"]},{"k":"li","s":["Santa Elena"]},{"k":"li","s":["Guayas"]},{"k":"li","s":["El Oro"]},{"k":"li","s":["Los Ríos"]},{"k":"li","s":["Santo Domingo de los Tsáchilas"]},{"k":"p","s":["This does not apply to airside transit within Guayaquil Airport in Guayas province."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Within 20km of the Ecuador-Colombia border"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to areas within 20km of the Ecuador-Colombia border, except for these areas in Carchi province:"]},{"k":"li","s":["El Ángel Ecological Reserve"]},{"k":"li","s":["Rumichaca border crossing"]},{"k":"li","s":["the town of Tulcán"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Pan-American Highway"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["why FCDO advises against travel","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ecuador/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Flooding on Santa Cruz Island, Galapagos"]},{"k":"p","s":["Heavy rains have caused flooding on Santa Cruz Island, prompting ",["Gobierno Galapagos","https://x.com/CGGalapagos"]," to declare an emergency on 31 October. Road closures, power outages, and disruptions to travel to Baltra Airport have been reported."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should follow the instructions of the local authorities, avoid flooded areas, and confirm travel arrangements with airlines or tour operators."]},{"k":"p","s":["Local authorities encourage tourists on Santa Cruz Island to complete the ",["form","https://form.jotform.com/230545460190652"]," (available only in Spanish) indicating whether they need urgent assistance and what their travel plans are."]},{"k":"h","s":["State of Emergency"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Ecuadorean government has declared a 60-day state of emergency as of 15 September, for security reasons in the following 8 provinces:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Guayas"]},{"k":"li","s":["Manabí"]},{"k":"li","s":["Santa Elena"]},{"k":"li","s":["Los Ríos"]},{"k":"li","s":["El Oro"]},{"k":"li","s":["Pichincha"]},{"k":"li","s":["Esmeraldas"]},{"k":"li","s":["Santo Domingo de los Tsáchilas"]},{"k":"p","s":["The state of emergency includes the following cantons:"]},{"k":"li","s":["La Maná (Cotopaxi province)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Las Naves (Bolívar province)"]},{"k":"li","s":["Camilo Ponce Enriquez (Azuay province)"]},{"k":"p","s":["The State of Emergency will mean greater police and military presence on the streets. It includes curfew hours (from 12am to 5am) for the provinces of El Oro, Guayas, Los Rios and Manabi, from 17 to 30 September. Follow the advice of local authorities and monitor local media."]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ecuador/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Ecuador’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Ecuador set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Ecuadorean Embassy in the UK","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/foreign-embassies-in-the-uk"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Documents required to enter via land or river border"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you wish to enter Ecuador via a land or river border from Peru or Colombia, you must present a criminal record certificate from the country where you lived for the last 5 years. The certificate must be legalised with the Hague Apostille and translated into Spanish if issued in another language. This mandatory requirement was announced on 11 January 2024 for implementation with immediate effect."]},{"k":"p","s":["Alternatively, if you do not have a legalised criminal record certificate, you can ask the Ecuadorean immigration authorities at the border to carry out a check on their ’SIMIEC’ Ecuadorean Immigration System (which flags any travel prohibitions or INTERPOL alerts). The following people are exempt from the presentation of the criminal record certificate:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Holders of a valid Ecuadorean visa"]},{"k":"li","s":["Holders of diplomatic passports"]},{"k":"li","s":["Children and adolescents under 18"]},{"k":"li","s":["Victims of human trafficking or those seeking international protection under the Ecuadorean Human Mobility Law."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are no restrictions in place for anyone flying into Ecuador."]},{"k":"p","s":["To obtain a UK Police Criminal Record Certificate, ",["check the ACRO website","https://www.acro.police.uk/s/acro-services/police-certificates"],". For information about legalising the document with the Hague apostille check the ",["Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office’s Legalisation Office website","https://www.gov.uk/get-document-legalised"]," site."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Ecuador, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can visit Ecuador without a visa for up to 90 days in any 12-month period."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re already in Ecuador and want to stay longer than 90 days, you can pay to ",["extend your stay","https://servicio.migracion.gob.ec/tramite/3"]," (in Spanish) by an extra 90 days. You must apply for an extension before the first 90-day period ends or you could be fined."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you overstay your visa, you will get a fine when you leave. You will not be allowed to return to Ecuador unless you pay the fine before you leave the country."]},{"k":"p","s":["For immigration information, see ",["Ecuador Migration","https://www.migracion.gob.ec/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer, to work or study, for business travel or for other reasons, you must meet the Ecuadorean government’s entry ",["requirements","https://www.cancilleria.gob.ec/2020/06/15/visas-en-el-ecuador/"]," (in Spanish)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ecuador’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs and Human Mobility (MFAHM) has an ",["e-visa application process","https://serviciosdigitales.cancilleria.gob.ec/authentication"]," (in Spanish). Find the ",["relevant MFAHM district zone offices","https://www.cancilleria.gob.ec/2020/07/30/direccioneszonales/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For general guidance contact ssmc@cancilleria.gob.ec or call (02) 299 3200 extension 23064."]},{"k":"h","s":["Checks at border control"]},{"k":"p","s":["All travellers entering Ecuador must register their entry and exit in the national immigration database. This is a mandatory requirement under Ecuadorean immigration regulations. At border control, you may be asked to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["provide your reason for travel"]},{"k":"li","s":["show proof of a return or onward journey"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Peru and Colombia land border crossings"]},{"k":"p","s":["On the northern border with Colombia, the Rumichaca checkpoint is open. On the southern border with Peru, the Huaquillas, Macará, Zapotillo, and Lalamor checkpoints are open to international travellers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Other checkpoints remain closed until further notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are entering Ecuador by land from Peru or Colombia, it is essential to use an official immigration checkpoint to receive the required passport entry stamp indicating your arrival date. Some buses may bypass immigration checkpoints, which can result in travellers not receiving the necessary stamp. This can lead to serious immigration issues."]},{"k":"p","s":["Evading immigration control filters, regardless of the reason, is considered a serious offence. According to official sources:"]},{"k":"li","s":["it will result in denied entry (‘inadmisión’) on future entry attempts"]},{"k":"li","s":["immigration fines are generally not applicable for this type of violation (but they apply for overstaying cases)"]},{"k":"li","s":["for official enquiries or direct assistance, contact an ",["immigration office","https://servicio.migracion.gob.ec/"]," (in Spanish) as soon as possible"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Galapagos Islands"]},{"k":"p","s":["Tourists can stay in the Galapagos Islands for up to 60 days. To enter Galapagos, you must have:"]},{"k":"li","s":["evidence of a hotel booking or an invitation letter from your host"]},{"k":"li","s":["evidence of a return flight to the Ecuadorean mainland"]},{"k":"li","s":[["purchase a transit control card","https://siiws.gobiernogalapagos.gob.ec/siicgg_web/"]," at your departure airport before checking in for your flight – you must also keep a copy"]},{"k":"li","s":["travel health insurance, which is mandatory for foreign tourists"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are travelling to or from Galapagos Islands via Guayaquil airport you should check with the airline before booking to ensure you will remain airside at Guayaquil airport. FCDO advises against all but essential travel to Guayaquil, except for airside transit through the airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check the ",["Ministry of Tourism’s accredited tourism service providers","https://www.observatoriogalapagos.gob.ec/directorio-servicios-regularizados-galapagos"]," (in Spanish), which includes information on accommodation, transport, tour operators and restaurants."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travelling with children"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Leaving Ecuador"]},{"k":"p","s":["British children, including British - Ecuadorean dual nationals, aged 17 and under must have a notarised consent letter (in Spanish) to leave Ecuador if they are:"]},{"k":"li","s":["travelling alone or with one parent or legal guardian"]},{"k":"li","s":["resident in Ecuador or have been in Ecuador for over 90 days, even if they are travelling on a British passport"]},{"k":"p","s":["If they are tourists (not resident in Ecuador) British children (or British-Ecuadorean dual nationals) do not need a notarised consent letter (Art.137 Human Mobility Organic Law’ 2017)."]},{"k":"p","s":["If one parent is deceased, the other parent must submit the death certificate to an Ecuadorean public notary to get an indefinite notarial permit to travel with the child."]},{"k":"p","s":["In more complex situations, such as legal disputes, a child will need judicial written permission (‘Autorización de Viaje Judicial’) issued by an Ecuadorian judge (‘Juzgado de la Niñez y Adolescencia’)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Entering Ecuador"]},{"k":"p","s":["Children entering Ecuador with someone other than a parent or legal guardian do not need a consent letter."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Ecuador guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/70/ecuador#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["From 29 July, every adult passenger or family group travelling to or from Ecuador carrying taxable goods or cash equal to or higher than 10,000 US dollars must:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["register online","https://www.aduana.gob.ec/servicio-al-ciudadano/formulario-de-registro-aduanero/login/"]]},{"k":"li","s":["complete the form, and present the digital version (QR code) to customs on arrival/departure (airports, land borders, ports)"]},{"k":"p","s":["This form must be completed at least three days before travelling. Failure to declare goods can lead to fines, confiscation of goods or criminal charges."]},{"k":"h","s":["Taking money out of Ecuador"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must declare cash you’re carrying when you leave Ecuador. You will have to pay tax on amounts over 1,350 US dollars."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ecuador/warnings-and-insurance"]," and ",["regional risks advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ecuador/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Ecuador"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Ecuador cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Due to the current State of Emergency, expect heightened police and military presence nationwide without previous notice, and potential travel disruptions due to extra security checks."]},{"k":"p","s":["Exercise caution around large gatherings. Street demonstrations, protests and strikes are common and can occur without warning. While most are peaceful, they can turn violent. You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["stay alert and monitor local media"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid large gatherings"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow updates from the ",["ECU 911 emergency services","https://twitter.com/ecu911_"]]},{"k":"li","s":["be wary of unverified information"]},{"k":"li","s":["allow extra time to reach your destination"]},{"k":"li","s":[["check road conditions","https://www.ecu911.gob.ec/consulta-de-vias/"]," (in Spanish) on the Ecuadorean government website"]},{"k":"p","s":["Even peaceful protests can lead to travel disruption."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Mugging and pickpocketing are common, take care in Quito’s old town and at transport hubs. The methods criminals use include:"]},{"k":"li","s":["distraction techniques (for example, requests for assistance, squirting liquids on to you, staged fights and pushing or shoving)"]},{"k":"li","s":["bag snatching at knife or gunpoint by a passenger on a motorbike"]},{"k":"p","s":["To reduce your risk:"]},{"k":"li","s":["stay alert in public places"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid walking alone in quiet areas or at night"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not wear expensive jewellery in public"]},{"k":"li","s":["carry only the money you need for the day"]},{"k":"li","s":["take care of your credit cards"]},{"k":"li","s":["watch your bags on public transport and wear your rucksack on the front of your body"]},{"k":"li","s":["carry a colour copy of your passport, including the visa entry stamp page – keep the original safe"]},{"k":"li","s":["use a money belt or secure inside pocket to keep your passport, credit cards and money safe"]},{"k":"p","s":["For more information on general travel safety in Ecuador, ",["check the Ministry of Tourism website","https://ecuador.travel/en/travel-safe/"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Violent crime and armed robbery"]},{"k":"p","s":["On 14 October 2025, a car bomb exploded near the Mall del Sol shopping centre and the Sheraton Hotel in northern Guayaquil, resulting in multiple injuries and at least one fatality. Secondary explosions were reported on 15 October 2025 on a road connecting the provinces of Guayas and El Oro."]},{"k":"p","s":["Armed robbery is a risk throughout Ecuador. Crimes with the threat of violence, such as gunpoint robbery and home invasion, can happen throughout Ecuador."]},{"k":"p","s":["Take particular care in Esmeraldas, Guayas and Manabi provinces (currently the FCDO advises against all but essential travel to those provinces) and get local advice on where to visit. Armed thieves have stopped vehicles and threatened passengers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Most violent crime is gang-related, but tourists can be affected. The murder rate in Guayaquil is very high, but is mainly gang-related. Be particularly cautious in Guayaquil city centre, southern parts of the city and port areas."]},{"k":"p","s":["Since March 2023, organised crime gangs have caused small explosions and made false bomb threats in Quito and Guayaquil. If you’re near an incident, follow the instructions of police and local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["Get local advice about the area you’re visiting and travel in a group when possible."]},{"k":"hs","s":["ATM and bank customer robbery"]},{"k":"p","s":["Take care when withdrawing money from a bank or ATM. Where possible use ATMs inside banks or shopping centres, and avoid withdrawing money after dark. There have been violent robberies outside banks. The Ecuadorean national police offers a free escort service if you are withdrawing or depositing large quantities of cash. To request this service, call 911. Only take out as much money as you need."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Transport robbery"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of robbery and pickpocketing on interstate transport and at bus stations, including on routes commonly used by tourists. To reduce your risk:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid road travel after dark"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep valuables safe in a money belt or inside pocket"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not store bags in overhead luggage spaces or under your seat"]},{"k":"p","s":["Criminals can pose as uniformed bus inspectors in some Quito bus stations. They might insist you place your luggage overhead so an accomplice can steal it later. You do not have to place belongings overhead."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are robbed, do not resist attackers or do anything that puts you at greater risk. Report the incident to the police as soon as possible."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sexual assault"]},{"k":"p","s":["Serious sexual assaults and attacks against foreign women have happened in Ecuador, particularly in the tourist beach town of Montañita in Santa Elena Province. Check reviews of your accommodation to make sure it is reputable and secure, even if you’re travelling in a group. Avoid travel after dark and be alert to the use of date rape and other drugs in drinks. If you feel unwell, seek urgent help from the police or nearest health centre. Call 911 for the emergency services."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["information for victims of rape and sexual assault in Ecuador","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ecuador-information-for-victims-of-rape-and-sexual-assault"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can ",["report gender-based violence","https://www.fiscalia.gob.ec/denuncia-en-linea-violencia-contra-la-mujer/"]," to the Prosecutor Office (‘Fiscalía’) online."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Criminal kidnap"]},{"k":"p","s":["Kidnapping rates have risen in Ecuador. Express kidnappings – short term opportunistic abductions, aimed at extracting cash from the victim – are common throughout Ecuador. Victims can be targeted or selected at random. They are held while criminals withdraw money from their bank accounts using their cash cards. Many cases involve victims who have hailed taxis (registered or illegal) from the street. In some cases, victims are subdued with scopolamine (causing amnesia). Be aware of your surroundings and always let someone you trust know your whereabouts, especially after dark."]},{"k":"p","s":["Business people and visitors perceived to be wealthy may be targeted by kidnappers in search of pay-outs. Be discreet about discussing your financial or business affairs in places where you may be overheard by others, and when arranging meetings use a secure location."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are a victim of kidnapping, follow the kidnapper’s instructions."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drug-assisted robbery"]},{"k":"p","s":["Criminals may use drugs to incapacitate victims and facilitate robbery. Homemade versions of the drug scopolamine can disorient individuals and impair their ability to resist or remember events. Scopolamine can be absorbed through the skin and may be applied to your hand, face or body without you realising."]},{"k":"p","s":["Remain cautious if approached by strangers offering items such as food, drinks, leaflets or perfume samples, even if they appear friendly or well-dressed. Some British nationals have reported incidents involving this type of drug in private rented accommodation. You should exercise caution when inviting unfamiliar people into your home or rented accommodation."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Methanol poisoning"]},{"k":"p","s":["There has been an increase in cases of serious illness caused by alcoholic drinks containing methanol in popular travel destinations around the world."]},{"k":"p","s":["Even small amounts of methanol can kill. It is not possible to identify methanol in alcoholic drinks by taste or smell. See ",["Travel Aware Spiking and methanol poisoning","https://travelaware.campaign.gov.uk/spiking-and-methanol-poisoning/"]," for information about how to reduce the risks."]},{"k":"p","s":["Seek urgent medical attention if you or someone you are travelling with show the ",["signs of methanol poisoning","https://travelaware.campaign.gov.uk/spiking-and-methanol-poisoning/"]," after drinking."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["By law you must carry ID. Always carry a printed copy of your passport, including the pages with your photograph and Ecuadorean entry stamp."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs and prison sentences"]},{"k":"p","s":["The penalties for drug trafficking or drug use are harsh, and prison conditions are very basic."]},{"k":"hs","s":["‘Spiritual cleansing’ hallucinogens"]},{"k":"p","s":["In Ecuador people use traditional hallucinogens such as Ayahuasca or San Pedro. These substances are marketed to tourists for ‘spiritual cleansing’ rituals. They often contain dimethyltryptamine (DMT), a strong hallucinogen that is illegal in the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are many risks when taking these substances including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["serious illness and death"]},{"k":"li","s":["assault and robbery while intoxicated"]},{"k":"li","s":["lack of nearby medical help as these ceremonies often take place in remote locations"]},{"k":"p","s":["Consumption of traditional hallucinogens is unregulated."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex relations are legal in Ecuador. However, local attitudes towards the LGBT+ community can be conservative and there is still societal discrimination. People may be less tolerant of same-sex couples showing affection in public than they are in the UK. Showing affection may attract negative attention, especially in small towns. Transgender people in particular could face unwanted attention."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Money"]},{"k":"p","s":["US dollars and 5, 10, 25 and 50 centavos coins minted in Ecuador are the only legal currencies. Credit cards and travellers cheques are generally accepted in cities but not elsewhere in the country."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Volunteer and adventure activities"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you join a volunteer or adventure expedition programme, make sure the UK organisation responsible for travel has an official agent in Ecuador able to handle an emergency. Be wary of unauthorised intermediaries (‘enganchadores’) trying to offer you cheap hotels or tour deals."]},{"k":"p","s":["British nationals have died while rafting, kayaking and on canopy walks and zip-lines. Make sure you’re fit enough for these activities and others such as bungee jumping and quad biking. For water adventure sports, make sure the weather conditions and river currents are safe."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["only use reputable tour operators with a specific licence to provide these services"]},{"k":"li","s":["be aware equipment may not meet UK safety and insurance standards"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the company uses the most up-to-date equipment and safety features"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the company is properly insured"]},{"k":"li","s":["make sure your insurance covers all your activities"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Swimming safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a risk of drowning caused by strong, rapidly changing ocean currents. Beaches do not always have swimming safety warning flags. Seek local advice on tidal activity."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hiking and mountaineering"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re hiking or mountaineering in Ecuador, prepare for high altitude and unpredictable climates. Hire an accredited guide or tour operator for these activities."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["climb at a moderate rate so your body can adjust"]},{"k":"li","s":["stay well hydrated"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep to established paths or use an experienced guide"]},{"k":"li","s":["make sure someone knows where you’re going and when you plan to return"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid walking alone"]},{"k":"p","s":["Lightning has killed tourists climbing mountains. Watch the weather and reconsider your plans if conditions look bad. Turn off any electronic devices if you encounter a thunderstorm; this prevents electrical surges, stops devices from acting as lightning rods, and protects you from close-range strikes."]},{"k":"p","s":["For more information on climbing or mountaineering, see the ",["Ministry of Environment","https://www.ambiente.gob.ec/"],", [Ecuador Tourism] (https://www.turismo.gob.ec/), and ",["Chimborazo Local Government","https://twitter.com/goberchimborazo"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For accredited guides, check ",["the Ecuadorean Mountaineering Accredited Guides Association","https://aseguim.org/"]," (in Spanish)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Climbing mountains with glaciers"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to do high mountaineering or climb a glaciated mountain without an official accredited guide."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Pichincha volcano"]},{"k":"p","s":["Tourists take day trips to Pichincha volcano on the cable car (‘teleférico’) from Quito. Cruz Loma arrival station is at 4.050 metres above sea level. There have been accidents on the route to one of the peaks, Rucu Pichincha, including deaths from hypothermia. You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["be aware of the risk of altitude sickness"]},{"k":"li","s":["take warm, waterproof clothing and high-factor sunblock"]},{"k":"li","s":["go with an accredited specialised guide who knows the route"]},{"k":"li","s":["start early to reduce risks from unexpected heavy mist or storms"]},{"k":"li","s":["wear a helmet if climbing up the peak Rucu Pichincha"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Cayambe Volcano"]},{"k":"p","s":["There may be access restrictions for Cayambe volcano depending on the weather and glacier conditions. Comply with safety regulations."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Chimborazo Reserve"]},{"k":"p","s":["To visit Chimborazo National Reserve, you must email chimborazo@ambiente.gob.ec at least 5 days in advance to register on the Biodiversity Information System (‘Sistema de Información de Biodiversidad’). The Chimborazo National Reserve is only open for tour operators and accredited mountaineering clubs after an avalanche killed 3 climbers in 2021."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Cotopaxi National Park"]},{"k":"p","s":["High mountaineering in Cotopaxi National Park is restricted. Since November 2023, tour operators, mountaineering clubs and individual visitors can go high mountaineering in Cotopaxi National Park. However, they must comply with mandatory national park rules and safety regulations."]},{"k":"p","s":["For information, see the ",["Ecuadorean Ministry of Environment","https://twitter.com/ambiente_ec"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re visiting Cotopaxi National Park, carry:"]},{"k":"li","s":["sunglasses and a hat"]},{"k":"li","s":["clothing that covers your skin"]},{"k":"li","s":["enough water and food"]},{"k":"li","s":["an emergency kit"]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Ecuador, see information on ",["driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can use a UK photocard driving licence to drive in Ecuador for the first 6 months after you arrive. If you still have a paper UK driving licence, you may need to ",["update it to a photocard licence","https://www.gov.uk/exchange-paper-driving-licence"]," or get the ",["correct version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," as well. After 6 months, you need to take an Ecuadorean driving test to get an Ecuadorean driving licence or carry out the driving licence exchange process."]},{"k":"p","s":["Always carry your passport, driving licence and vehicle registration with you in the vehicle."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as 6 months of driving experience, a minimum age of 18 years old and holding an IDP."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some cities do not allow vehicles with Ecuadorean plates to drive on certain days or at peak times based on the last digits of their car’s registration plate. Check for restrictions before you drive. You could be fined if you do not follow the rules."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ecuador has one of the highest rates of road accidents in Latin America due to careless driving, speeding and badly maintained vehicles. There have been many deaths, including British nationals."]},{"k":"p","s":["In Ecuador:"]},{"k":"li","s":["road conditions vary"]},{"k":"li","s":["heavy rains and mudslides often cause roads to be closed or wash away"]},{"k":"li","s":["driving can be erratic – be prepared to stop unexpectedly"]},{"k":"li","s":["vehicles may move slowly, change lanes without indicating or jump red lights"]},{"k":"li","s":["many drivers are uninsured"]},{"k":"li","s":["drivers usually ignore zebra crossings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always wear a seat belt. If you’re a passenger in a speeding vehicle, ask the driver to slow down."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Book taxis through your hotel or by calling a known radio-dispatched taxi service. Travel in a group if possible. Using unregistered taxis significantly increases your risk of becoming a victim of crime."]},{"k":"p","s":["While taxi service apps do exist and provide the driver’s details, the services themselves are unregulated in Ecuador."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re using a yellow registered taxi in Quito or Guayaquil, make sure it has:"]},{"k":"li","s":["a municipality registration number sticker on the windscreen and doors"]},{"k":"li","s":["orange licence plates or the new white plates with an orange strip on the top"]},{"k":"li","s":["video cameras inside"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are authorised taxi booths at Quito and Guayaquil international airports, and potentially at other domestic airports."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re using a yellow registered taxi in Quito, make sure the driver resets the meter. In Guayaquil and other cities, taxis use fixed prices rather than meters. In these places, or if you or your hotel calls a taxi in Quito, agree a price before you get in."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sea travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["There has been piracy and armed robbery against ships in and around Ecuador’s waters. Sailors should stay alert and take appropriate precautions."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling by sea, ",["read about the regional risks at sea","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/sea-river-and-piracy-safety"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been several serious accidents in the Galapagos Islands involving tour company boats and smaller vessels transporting people between islands. Some of the smaller boats do not follow safety measures, such as holding the right licence, respecting maximum capacity for passengers, checking their motor or fuel and providing enough life vests. Use reputable boat operators and ask about safety features before you book, even for short trips. Check they provide life vests and lifeboats (if appropriate) before boarding."]},{"k":"h","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to prepare for and respond to ",["extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["2026–2027 El Niño"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Ecuadorean authorities have issued a red alert ahead of the forecast 2026-2027 El Niño weather event."]},{"k":"p","s":["El Niño is expected to generate unusually heavy rainfall, possible flooding and landslides, and higher temperatures, which may disrupt travel and other services."]},{"k":"p","s":["Extreme weather may affect your travel plans. Monitor local news and weather reports and follow the advice of local authorities. For official updates and forecasts, see the Ecuadorean government’s ",["El Niño information website","https://script.google.com/macros/s/AKfycbz6HA9kKLeksqm49YYPD7i3A40mMK6JwmoDc50vaiv4myVTk0lFYswqnE4IbJXmr9Lm/exec"]," (in Spanish)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Landslides"]},{"k":"p","s":["Landslides are common in Ecuador and can have a serious impact on travel. Heavy rains can lead to landslides, which frequently lead to road closures. Check the weather forecast if you are travelling by road."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high risk of earthquakes across Ecuador. Earthquakes and aftershocks can cause landslides, which block roads."]},{"k":"p","s":["Familiarise yourself with safety procedures and any instructions in your hotel. If there is an earthquake, follow instructions from the authorities and keep essential belongings, such as your passport and money, with you."]},{"k":"p","s":["Get information (in Spanish) following an earthquake from the:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Ecuadorean National Geophysical Institute"]},{"k":"li","s":["Secretariat for Risk Management (Secretaría Nacional de Gestión de Riesgos) – check current alerts"]},{"k":"li","s":[["ECU 911 emergency services","https://www.ecu911.gob.ec/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["The US Federal Emergency Management Agency website has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after an earthquake","https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can also ",["get earthquake alerts with an Android phone","https://support.google.com/android/answer/9319337?hl=en-GB"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tsunamis"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high risk of tsunamis along the coast and in the Galapagos Islands. If there is a tsunami alert, sirens will sound in Esmeraldas, El Oro, Guayas, Santa Elena, Manabí and Galapagos. See ",["evacuation route maps","https://www.gestionderiesgos.gob.ec/biblioteca/"]," and follow the advice of the authorities."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Rainy season"]},{"k":"p","s":["The rainy season usually runs from October to May but can last until June."]},{"k":"p","s":["During heavy rainy seasons:"]},{"k":"li","s":["monitor local media"]},{"k":"li","s":["check your itinerary with your tour operator to avoid disruption"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid river crossings due to potential strong currents"]},{"k":"li","s":["take care in affected areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["The weather can change quickly. Check ",["weather forecasts","https://twitter.com/inamhi_ec"],". Get local advice on tides if you’re near the coast."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re climbing or mountaineering, get official advice from the authorities and tour operators."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Forest fires"]},{"k":"p","s":["Forest fires happen in many areas, especially in Pichincha, Loja, Azuay and Cotopaxi provinces , due to high temperatures, strong winds, dry conditions and arson. Call 911 if you see a fire."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Volcanic eruptions"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are many volcanoes in the highlands of Ecuador and the Galapagos Islands. Some are currently erupting, active or potentially active. Check the ",["current volcano alerts","https://www.igepn.edu.ec/red-de-observatorios-vulcanologicos-rovig"]," (in Spanish). Follow instructions issued by the local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ashfall from volcanoes can disrupt flights and close airports at short notice. Before going to the airport, check with your airline and on the airport website. Ash is also a health hazard, especially for travellers with respiratory problems."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high risk from mixed flows of water, mud, lava and debris (‘lahars’) around glaciated volcanoes, including Cotopaxi. These flows can be very destructive. The towns of Latacunga and Salcedo and low-lying areas in the valley to the east of Quito (Los Chillos and Rumiñahui) are particularly vulnerable."]},{"k":"p","s":["Access to the Reventador volcano (Napo province) is currently restricted."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Ecuador. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ecuador"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ecuador/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Coastal regions"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the Coastal Region provinces of:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Esmeraldas"]},{"k":"li","s":["Manabi"]},{"k":"li","s":["Santa Elena"]},{"k":"li","s":["El Oro"]},{"k":"li","s":["Los Ríos"]},{"k":"li","s":["Santo Domingo de los Tsáchilas"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is due to a high level of gang-related violence linked to the presence of organised crime relating to the production and trafficking of illegal drugs."]},{"k":"p","s":["Tourists are not usually targeted. However, you could be a victim of violence due to mistaken identity or be caught up in a security incident involving others."]},{"k":"h","s":["Guayas"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the Coastal Region province of Guayas, except:"]},{"k":"li","s":["airside transit within Guayaquil Airport in Guayas province."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are travelling to or from the Galápagos Islands via Guayaquil airport you should check with the airline before booking to ensure you will remain airside at Guayaquil Airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["This is due to a high level of gang-related violence linked to the presence of organised crime relating to the production and trafficking of illegal drugs."]},{"k":"p","s":["There are instances of express kidnapping, where individuals have been kidnapped for a short period of time, and are often driven to an ATM to withdraw money and then abandoned."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been several security incidents in and around the city of Guayaquil in Guayas province since 2023 including an increase in murders and small explosions. There have also been fatal armed attacks against the police and prosecutors."]},{"k":"p","s":["Tourists are not usually targeted. However, you could be a victim of violence due to mistaken identity or caught up in a security incident involving others."]},{"k":"h","s":["Within 20km of the Ecuador-Colombia border"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to areas within 20km of the Ecuador-Colombia border, except for these areas in Carchi province:"]},{"k":"li","s":["El Ángel Ecological Reserve"]},{"k":"li","s":["Rumichaca border crossing"]},{"k":"li","s":["the town of Tulcán"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Pan-American Highway"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is due to the presence of organised crime linked to the production and trafficking of drugs."]},{"k":"p","s":["The security situation can change quickly in areas outside the 20km border zone, including Esmeraldas, Carchi and Sucumbíos provinces. There are illegal armed groups and criminal gangs in these areas. If you are travelling to these areas, including Cuyabeno, the Napo River, and the El Ángel Ecological Reserve, where there are ecolodges, you should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["stay alert and take local advice"]},{"k":"li","s":["pay close attention to warnings issued by the Ecuadorean authorities"]},{"k":"li","s":["travel during daylight hours with a reputable, official guide"]},{"k":"li","s":["have an emergency plan and good communication systems in place"]},{"k":"li","s":["be aware some lodges are far from a major hospital"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you cross the northern border at Tulcán (Rumichaca official land border point), enter and exit the town on the Pan-American Highway."]},{"k":"p","s":["Lago Agrio (also known as Nueva Loja) the main town in Sucumbíos province, and San Lorenzo, in Esmeraldas province, are both within the 20km border zone where FCDO advises against all but essential travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 911 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can get emergency assistance and register medical issues using the ",["ECU 911 smartphone application","https://www.ecu911.gob.ec/app-ecu-911/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Ecuador","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/70/ecuador#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Ecuador","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/70/ecuador"],", including Oropouche virus."]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Ecuador. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Ultraviolet radiation is a risk in Ecuador. Read more about ",["sun protection on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/23/sun-protection"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drinking water"]},{"k":"p","s":["To avoid getting sick, drink boiled, filtered or bottled water and avoid ice in drinks."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Pharmacies in Ecuador may accept some UK prescriptions. They will accept antimicrobial prescriptions up to 3 days old and narcotic or psychotropic prescriptions up to 5 days old."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Ecuador"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Ecuadorean public healthcare system provides the same level of care to locals and foreigners free of charge. However, it is currently facing challenges, including delays in obtaining appointments and limited access to medicines, due to funding constraints, increased demand and potential staff shortages. Private treatment can be very expensive, and private hospitals require a credit card guarantee for admission. Healthcare standards and access to specialised treatment can vary, and may be limited outside major cities."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["medical providers in Ecuador","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ecuador-list-of-hospitals--2"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["hospitalisation information pack","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ecuador-hospitalisation-information-pack"]," for further details on healthcare in Ecuador, including a ",["list of hospitals","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ecuador-list-of-hospitals"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Healthcare in the Galapagos Islands"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are limited medical facilities in the Galapagos Islands. You should ensure that your travel insurance includes air ambulance evacuation."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you travel to the Galapagos Islands by boat, you may be asked to supply information such as your blood group and emergency contacts when you board."]},{"k":"p","s":["Healthcare provision varies across the islands. San Cristóbal has a relatively well-equipped public hospital, although it may face the same constraints as mainland facilities. Santa Cruz has a basic hospital that is currently undergoing refurbishment. Isabela Island only has a very small and extremely basic health centre."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],". You can find information about ",["mental health support for British nationals in Ecuador.","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ecuador-mental-health-support-for-british-nationals/ecuador-mental-health-support-for-british-nationals"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Ecuador"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 911 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Tourism police"]},{"k":"p","s":["Quito has a tourism police unit with branches in the north, old town, airport and at bus terminals. There are also tourism police units in the cities of Guayaquil, Cuenca, Santa Elena and Galapagos provinces. Read more ",["information on the tourism police from their website","https://www.policia.gob.ec/policia-turismo/"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Reporting robbery and theft online"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can ",["report robbery and theft online in English","https://www.fiscalia.gob.ec/denuncias-en-linea-para-turistas/"]," to the Ecuador Attorney General’s Office (‘Fiscalía General’). This report is valid for insurance claims, however, once you leave Ecuador, following up a case report through this link may be complicated."]},{"k":"h","s":["Complaining about tourism services"]},{"k":"p","s":["To complain about tourism services, email the Ministry of Tourism: denuncias@produccion.gob.ec."]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=ecuador"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=ecuador"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=ecuador"]," in Ecuador"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Ecuador","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ecuador-bereavement-information-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Ecuador","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ecuador-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/ecuador/british-embassy-quito"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ecuador/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Ecuador","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-ecuador"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":38493,"change":"Updated information about heavy rain and flooding on Santa Cruz Island, Galapagos. (Warnings and insurance)"},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/estonia":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"4212bc3b67cb12272422e8f7e6b8b047ad84916d19fddf32c6a1e00db72e7a84","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/estonia","bytes":57141,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Estonia travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-01-07T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-11T10:18:21+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/estonia/email-signup"]," when this advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/travelaware/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of the current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Estonia set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Estonian Embassy in London","https://london.mfa.ee/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["Estonia follows ",["Schengen area rules","https://europa.eu/youreurope/citizens/travel/entry-exit/non-eu-nationals/index_en.htm"],". Your passport must:"]},{"k":"li","s":["have a ‘date of issue’ less than 10 years before the date you arrive – if you renewed your passport before 1 October 2018, it might have a date of issue that is more than 10 years ago, making it invalid for entry to the Schengen area"]},{"k":"li","s":["have an ‘expiry date’ at least 3 months after the day you plan to leave the Schengen area (the expiry date does not need to be within 10 years of the date of issue)"]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can ",["travel without a visa to the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/travel-to-eu-schengen-area"],", which includes Estonia, for up to 90 days in any 180-day period without a visa. This applies if you travel:"]},{"k":"li","s":["as a tourist"]},{"k":"li","s":["to visit family or friends"]},{"k":"li","s":["to attend business meetings, cultural or sports events"]},{"k":"li","s":["for short-term studies or training"]},{"k":"p","s":["The ",["requirements for working in Estonia","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/travel-to-estonia-for-work"]," are different."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling to other Schengen countries as well, make sure your whole visit is within the 90-day visa-free limit. Visits to Schengen countries within the previous 180 days before you travel count towards your 90 days. If you overstay the 90-day visa-free limit, you may be banned from entering Schengen countries for up to 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["At Estonian border control, you may also need to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["show a return or onward ticket"]},{"k":"li","s":["prove that you have enough money for your stay – the amount varies depending on your accommodation"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Staying longer than 90 days in a 180-day period"]},{"k":"p","s":["To stay longer than 90 days, check the ",["Estonian government’s entry requirements","https://www2.politsei.ee/en/teenused/eestis-viibimise-seaduslikud-alused/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re in Estonia with a residence permit or long-stay visa, this does not count towards your 90-day visa-free limit."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read about ",["entry processes if you live in Estonia","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/living-in-estonia#passports"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["New Schengen entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["The European Union’s (EU) new Entry/Exit System (EES) is now being implemented across the Schengen area."]},{"k":"p","s":["This means that when you travel into the Schengen area for short stays, you may need to register your biometric details, such as fingerprints and a photo. There is no cost for EES registration."]},{"k":"p","s":["On your first visit into a Schengen country, you may be asked to register your details at a special booth before proceeding to the immigration desk. Follow directions from your travel operator or the staff at your port of entry. You may also need to provide either your fingerprint or photo when you leave the Schengen area. Children aged 11 or younger will not have their fingerprints scanned but can be required to have their photo taken."]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need to take any action before you arrive at the border on entry to the Schengen area, but EES may take each passenger extra time to complete so be prepared to wait longer than usual at the border and to allow more time for immigration controls when you depart the Schengen area. If you are departing the Schengen area by plane, allow extra time to reach your departure gate as EES checks may take place before boarding."]},{"k":"p","s":["EES is replacing the previous system of manually stamping passports when visitors arrive in the Schengen area for short stays. You may be asked to input biometric details every time you enter or exit."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you enter the Schengen area through the Port of Dover, Eurotunnel at Folkestone or Eurostar at St Pancras International, any information will be taken at the border before you leave the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Your digital EES record is valid for 3 years."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["information on the EU Entry/Exit System and to understand which countries are in the Schengen area","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/eu-entryexit-system"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are long-term resident in a Schengen country, you are exempt from EES in all Schengen countries. The full list of EES exemptions is on ",["the EU’s official Travel Europe website.","https://travel-europe.europa.eu/en/ees/to-whom-does-ees-not-apply"]]},{"k":"p","s":["If you believe you have been incorrectly entered into EES or your record of time spent in the Schengen area is incorrect, approach border officials when you next cross the Schengen border and request a correction. If you are exempt, carry documentation confirming your status. If you believe your time spent in the Schengen area has been recorded incorrectly, raise this with border officials and be prepared to provide supporting evidence if requested."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel from Ukraine, Russia and Belarus"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are arriving in Estonia from Ukraine, Russia or Belarus, check the ",["Estonian government crisis website","https://kriis.ee/en"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel from Russia"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can reserve crossings and check the real-time ",["level of traffic at border crossing points from Russia","https://www.estonianborder.eu/yphis/borderQueueInfo.action"]," on Estonia’s ‘Go Swift’ website."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["For details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Estonia guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/75/estonia#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Estonia","https://www.emta.ee/en/private-client/consignments-travel-settlement/travel/declaration-goods"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking food and drink into Estonia"]},{"k":"p","s":["You cannot take ",["meat, milk or products containing them into EU countries","https://ec.europa.eu/food/animals/animalproducts/personal_imports_en"],". There are some exceptions such as powdered baby milk, baby food and special foods or pet feed required for medical reasons."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Restrictions on Russian vehicles"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are restrictions on vehicles with Russian licence plates in Estonia. See the ",["Estonian Ministry of the Interior’s website for more information","https://www.siseministeerium.ee/en/news/estonia-prohibits-entry-russian-motor-vehicles"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Estonia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Although there’s no recent history of terrorism in Estonia, attacks cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate including in places visited by foreign nationals. Stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Pickpockets can be a problem in bars and nightclubs in Tallinn’s Old Town."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drink spiking, alcohol and sexual assault"]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks or assault, including sexual assaults, are rare but do occur. The risk is highest late at night around popular nightlife locations. To reduce your personal risk:"]},{"k":"li","s":["save the location of your accommodation on your maps app, so it’s easier to find at the end of the night"]},{"k":"li","s":["set up a WhatsApp group to keep in touch with others in your group"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep an eye on each other’s drinks to make sure they do not get spiked"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not let a friend walk back to their hotel alone"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not give a drunk person more alcohol"]},{"k":"p","s":["Alcohol and drugs can reduce your vigilance, making you less in control. If you drink, know your limit. Drinks served in bars in Estonia are often stronger than those in the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["Report anything that does not feel right to local authorities or hotel or club management."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taxi safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always use pre-arranged taxis or official taxis with ‘TAKSO’ signage. Do not accept lifts from unmarked vehicles or with strangers."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you use an app, check that the car details match the car assigned to you. See ‘ ",["transport risks","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/estonia/safety-and-security"],"’ for more details."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Bank card skimming"]},{"k":"p","s":["Keep cards in sight when paying in shops and restaurants. Read more about ",["how to avoid card skimming","https://www.actionfraud.police.uk/a-z-of-fraud/bank-card-and-cheque-fraud"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Illegal drugs, including cannabis, are strictly prohibited, even in very small quantities. Severe penalties apply for possessing, using, buying, or smuggling illegal drugs, including when transiting through airports. You should expect long jail sentences of up to 10 years and heavy fines."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Estonia have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity is legal in Estonia, and same-sex marriages have been recognised since 1 January 2024."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Estonia, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"]," and check the ",["rules of the road in the RAC’s Estonia guide","https://www.rac.co.uk/drive/travel/country/estonia/"],". The guide lists driving regulations and other legal requirements you need to be aware of."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can drive in Estonia with a UK driving licence. You must have the original V5C vehicle registration document with you. Check for more details on the ",["Estonian state portal","https://www.eesti.ee/en/traffic/vehicles-and-right-to-drive/travel-documents-for-a-driver-of-a-motor-vehicle"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check if you need a ",["UK sticker to drive your car outside the UK","https://www.gov.uk/displaying-number-plates/flags-identifiers-and-stickers"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are visiting Estonia, you may be able to use your ",["Blue Badge","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/blue-badge-using-it-in-the-eu/using-a-blue-badge-in-the-european-union"]," in some circumstances. Check local parking rules."]},{"k":"p","s":["Hire car companies often have stricter requirements for their customers, such as a year of driving experience or a higher minimum age."]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not drink and drive. If you are tested and found to have more than a quarter of England’s legal limit of alcohol in your body, you may get a fine and possible imprisonment."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not hail unmarked taxis – they’re illegal. Make sure the taxi has:"]},{"k":"li","s":["a price list on the back window"]},{"k":"li","s":["the driver’s licence in a visible place"]},{"k":"li","s":["a working meter"]},{"k":"li","s":["‘TAKSO’ signage (for taxis at taxi ranks)"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can also use taxi apps like Bolt, Forus and Uber."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drone incidents"]},{"k":"p","s":["Some Ukrainian drones aimed at Russia have crossed into Estonian airspace. Follow official guidance as necessary."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dial 112 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance or medical assistance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Estonia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/75/estonia#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See what ",["health risks you’ll face in Estonia","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/75/estonia"],", including biting insects and ticks."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries. If you need advice about what you can take into Estonia, contact the ",["Estonian Medicine Agency","https://www.ravimiamet.ee/en/human-medicines/manufacture-distribution-pharmacy/travelling-medicinal-products"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Estonia"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a list of ",["medical providers in Estonia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/estonia-list-of-medical-facilitiespractitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Health insurance cards"]},{"k":"p","s":["To get medically necessary state healthcare in Estonia, you need a Global Health Insurance Card (GHIC) or a European Health Insurance Card (EHIC)."]},{"k":"p","s":["The NHS’s ",["getting healthcare abroad webpage","https://www.nhs.uk/using-the-nhs/healthcare-abroad/apply-for-a-free-uk-global-health-insurance-card-ghic/"]," has details about:"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to apply for a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to get temporary cover if you lose your card or it does not arrive in time"]},{"k":"li","s":["who qualifies for a new EHIC instead of a GHIC"]},{"k":"li","s":["what treatment counts as medically necessary"]},{"k":"p","s":["A GHIC or EHIC is not an alternative to travel insurance. You may have costs your GHIC or EHIC does not cover, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["changes to travel and accommodation bookings"]},{"k":"li","s":["additional standard costs for treatment"]},{"k":"li","s":["medical repatriation to the UK"]},{"k":"li","s":["treatment that is ruled non-urgent"]},{"k":"li","s":["private healthcare"]},{"k":"li","s":["private clinics"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is also ",["guidance on healthcare if you’re living in Estonia","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/healthcare-in-estonia"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Estonia"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 112 (ambulance, fire, police)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Estonia"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Estonia"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Estonia"]," in Estonia"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Estonia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/deathbereavement-information-sheet-in-tallinn"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested in Estonia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/estonia-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/estonia/british-embassy-tallinn"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/estonia/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Estonia","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-estonia"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":14690,"change":"Updated information on European Entry-Exit System (EES) (‘Entry requirements’ page)."},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/egypt":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"e3cdef358829fd628dbf84469ae32aa0a18d75d3872b9c8e49c5433369df6e8e","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/egypt","bytes":88234,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Egypt travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-18T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-10-27T16:03:56+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel insurance could be invalidated if you travel against advice from the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas where FCDO advises against travel"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Egypt-Libya border"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to within 20km of the Egypt-Libya border, except for the town of El Salloum, where we advise against all but essential travel."]},{"k":"hs","s":["North Sinai"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to the North Sinai Governorate."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Northern part of South Sinai"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the northern part of the South Sinai Governorate, beyond the St Catherine-Nuweibaa road, except for the coastal areas along the west and east of the peninsula."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Eastern part of Ismailiyah Governorate"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the Ismailiyah Governorate east of the Suez Canal."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Western Desert"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the area west of the Nile Valley and Nile Delta regions, except for:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Luxor, Qina, Aswan, Abu Simbel and the Valley of the Kings"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Faiyum Governorate"]},{"k":"li","s":["the coastal areas between the Nile Delta and Marsa Matruh"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Marsa Matruh-Siwa road"]},{"k":"li","s":["the oasis town of Siwa"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Giza Governorate north-east of the Bahariya Oasis"]},{"k":"li","s":["the White Desert and Black Desert"]},{"k":"li","s":["the oasis towns of Bahariya, Farafra, Dakhla (Mut) and Kharga"]},{"k":"li","s":["the following roads and the desert area between them and the Nile valley: the road between Giza and Farafra and within 50km either side of this road (but FCDO advises against all but essential travel on the road between Bahariya and Siwa)"]},{"k":"li","s":["the road between Farafra, Dakhla (Mut) and Kharga"]},{"k":"li","s":["the road between Kharga and Baris"]},{"k":"li","s":["the road between Baris and Luxor"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hala’ib Triangle and Bir Tawil Trapezoid"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the Hala’ib Triangle and the Bir Tawil Trapezoid."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["why FCDO advises against travel","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/egypt/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional tensions"]},{"k":"p","s":["The context in the Middle East remains deeply unpredictable and the risk of sudden escalation is high. Since conflict began in early 2026, Iran has stated its intention to target locations associated with the US and Israel, including, but not limited to, organisations, businesses, facilities, and institutions. There have been strikes and retaliatory attacks by Iran and others across the region against civilian and US military infrastructure."]},{"k":"h","s":["Conflict in Israel and Palestine"]},{"k":"p","s":["International borders in Israel and Palestine could close at short notice, including the Taba border crossing between Israel and Egypt. Check with local authorities and read ",["Israel travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/israel"]," and ",["Palestine travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/palestine"]," before trying to cross."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Entering Egypt from Gaza"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Rafah border crossing is currently closed. When re-opened, Israel, Egypt and the authorities in Gaza may determine procedures for who is permitted to cross and when."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are a British national in Gaza who wants to leave Gaza, see ",["Israel travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/israel#travelling-to-egypt-from-gaza"]," and ",["Palestine travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/palestine#travelling-to-egypt-from-gaza"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Concern for friends and family"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are concerned about friends or family, or need support from the UK government, call the British Embassy in Cairo."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: + 20 (0)2 2791 6000"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you have technical difficulties using this number, call +44 1767 667 600 (UK number)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/egypt/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Egypt’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Egypt set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Egyptian Consulate General in the UK","https://egyptconsulate.co.uk/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Egypt, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive and have at least one blank page."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["British passport holders travelling to Egypt normally need a visa."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are arriving by air to Sharm El-Sheikh and staying for less than 15 days at resorts in Sharm El Sheikh, Dahab, Nuweiba or Taba, you do not need a visa. You will get a free entry permission stamp on arrival at Sharm El-Sheikh Airport. If you plan to travel outside the resorts or stay more than 15 days, you must get a visa. You can get a visa at Sharm El-Sheikh Airport or online."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you arrive in South Sinai by land through the Taba Border Crossing, see ‘Entering Egypt from Israel’."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can apply for a visa:"]},{"k":"li","s":["online – ",["getting an e-visa in advance","https://visa2egypt.gov.eg/eVisa/Home?VISTK=AMEV-QAST-XKKB-4X4J-LYML-KSS1-J4VF-B3V5-GO2U-AYIZ-4F82-1WR5-RW7O-0L87-4CZ8-9CFV"]," can help to prevent delays, tourist e-visas are valid for up to 3 months"]},{"k":"li","s":["in person or by post – apply through the ",["Egyptian Consulate General in the UK","https://egyptconsulate.co.uk/visas/"]]},{"k":"li","s":["on arrival in Egypt – approved airports have kiosks before you reach the immigration counters where you can buy a physical visa for 30 US dollars (cash only). Visas on arrival are valid for up to 30 days."]},{"k":"p","s":["Starting from 1 August 2026, travellers arriving at Cairo International Airport can also purchase digital visas on arrival (card payment only) via self-service machines or the ",["Visa on Arrival portal","https://visaonarrival.gov.eg/home/landing"]," for 36 US dollars, including a 6 US dollar digital service fee."]},{"k":"p","s":["British passport holders from British Overseas Territories and Crown Dependencies cannot apply online."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Visa extensions"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can apply for a visa extension at the Egyptian Passport and Immigration Offices. If you try to leave Egypt on an expired visa, you may face difficulties, including a fine if your visa has expired by more than 14 days. Contact the ",["Egyptian Consulate General in the UK","https://egyptconsulate.co.uk/visas/"]," for more information."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Visa agents in airports"]},{"k":"p","s":["You do not need to buy a visa from an agent. Agents often charge more than the required 30 US dollars. If an agent harasses you, report the incident to the tourist police in the airport terminal."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Gender expression"]},{"k":"p","s":["You may be denied entry if your gender expression or physical appearance does not match the sex marker in your passport or your passport photograph. See ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Entering Egypt from Israel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan to cross the border from Israel into Egypt at Taba, we recommend you bring at least 170 US dollars (cash) per person, with some extra US dollars in cash to cover any further expenses. Since early March 2026, border crossing charges at Taba have been subject to increases at short notice."]},{"k":"li","s":["If staying in Taba, travellers will need to pay a border tax of 20 US dollars. The border tax allows you to obtain an entry permission stamp for a stay of up to 15 days"]},{"k":"li","s":["If travelling further than Taba, including to Sharm El-Sheikh, but remaining within the Sinai Peninsula, travellers will need to pay a border tax of 120 US dollars. The border tax allows you to obtain an entry permission stamp for a stay of up to 15 days"]},{"k":"li","s":["If travelling beyond the Sinai Peninsula, including to fly from airports such as Cairo and Hurghada, travellers will need to pay a border tax of 120 US dollars and purchase a standard entry visa for 30 US dollars. At Taba, this entry visa also requires a “letter of guarantee”, provided by travel agents and costing around 20 US dollars. Both the visa and the “letter of guarantee” can be purchased at the Border Crossing"]},{"k":"p","s":["If travelling by car through the Taba Border Crossing, you may also be required to pay 100 US dollars, with additional charges for parking."]},{"k":"p","s":["Please note that ATMs at the Taba Border Crossing are unreliable and frequently run out of cash. Travellers should obtain US dollars before travelling or in Eilat before arriving at the Crossing as all payments are required in cash."]},{"k":"p","s":["Border officials may ask travellers to remove hats, coats or other items of clothing, including religious clothing, as they pass through the Taba Border Crossing terminal."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Entering Egypt from Jordan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ferry services operate between Aqaba, Jordan and Taba Heights and Nuweiba in Egypt. Contact ferry operators directly for schedules and availability. Please check Travel Advice for Jordan for latest on exit requirements."]},{"k":"p","s":["If your stay in Sinai will exceed 15 days, or you are planning to travel in Egypt beyond the Sinai Peninsula, including to fly from mainland airports, for example Cairo or Hurghada, you will need an entry visa. If entering at Taba Heights, this costs 35 US dollars. If entering at Nuweiba, this costs 30 US dollars. Visas can be purchased at the port terminals. Travellers should obtain USD in cash before travelling given payments are often required in cash."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Entering Egypt from Sudan"]},{"k":"p","s":["The situation at the Egypt-Sudan border remains unclear. There are 2 main land border crossings, Qustul and Argeen. There is almost no infrastructure at these crossings, and it is no longer possible to obtain a visa on arrival at these locations. The Egyptian consulate is based in Wadi Halfa, 30km from the border, applications for Egyptian visas can be made here, but visa approvals can take upwards of 6 months."]},{"k":"p","s":["The British Embassy in Cairo does not have staff at the border and our ability to provide assistance is very limited. Travel in and from Sudan is at your own risk. See ",["Sudan travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/sudan"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are a British national and have crossed into Egypt from Sudan without valid documents, contact the ",["British Embassy in Cairo","https://www.gov.uk/world/organisations/british-embassy-cairo"]," for support."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Egypt, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Egypt guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/71/egypt#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["HIV test for work permits"]},{"k":"p","s":["You will need to show HIV test results to apply for a work permit."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Egypt","https://www.cairo-airport.com/en-us/Services/Passenger-Guide/Customs-Regulations"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty. Contact the ",["Egyptian Consulate General in the UK","https://egyptconsulate.co.uk/"]," for more information about customs rules."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into or out of Egypt"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can bring up to 5,000 Egyptian pounds into or out of Egypt."]},{"k":"p","s":["You must declare foreign currency worth more than 10,000 US dollars on arrival – there is no limit to the amount of foreign currency you can bring."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Electronic and communication devices"]},{"k":"p","s":["You should declare high value electronic devices and recording equipment when you arrive. Officials are likely to confiscate satellite phones or radio communications equipment unless you have prior clearance from the Ministry of Telecommunications."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["Approximately one million British people travel to Egypt each year. Most visits are trouble-free. This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of Egypt","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/egypt/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Egypt"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorists are likely to try to carry out attacks in Egypt."]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks could be indiscriminate including in places visited by foreign nationals. Stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities. Be vigilant in crowds and large gatherings."]},{"k":"p","s":["Targets might include:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Egyptian security forces"]},{"k":"li","s":["religious sites"]},{"k":"li","s":["large public gatherings"]},{"k":"li","s":["places visited by foreign nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Egypt have a significant security presence across the country. Extra measures are in place at tourist sites."]},{"k":"p","s":["Examples of recent attacks include:"]},{"k":"li","s":["In 2024, one Israeli-Canadian national was killed in a shooting attack in Alexandria"]},{"k":"li","s":["In 2023, 2 Israeli tourists and an Egyptian tour guide were killed in a shooting attack in Alexandria"]},{"k":"li","s":["In 2022, 4 police officers and one insurgent killed in an attack on a police checkpoint in Ismailiya City"]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["The political environment in Egypt is restrictive. Avoid large gatherings, demonstrations and protests as the situation could change quickly and without warning. Security forces have responded forcefully to protests and disturbances."]},{"k":"p","s":["Foreign nationals involved in any political activity or activities critical of the government may be at risk of detention. This includes posting negative comments on social media."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["The crime rate is generally low, but visitors should take sensible precautions as they would in the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["To report a crime, phone the tourist police on 126. If you do not report a crime before you leave Egypt, it will be difficult to prosecute later."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sexual assault and harassment"]},{"k":"p","s":["A number of sexual assaults have been reported to the British Embassy, including cases involving minors."]},{"k":"p","s":["The majority of cases that have been reported took place in tourist resorts in the Red Sea region, often committed by someone the victim had already met, including hotel workers and excursion staff. There have also been reports of hotel staff discouraging incidents being reported to local police."]},{"k":"p","s":["Female travellers should exercise caution when travelling alone, particularly at night, in taxis."]},{"k":"p","s":["Take extra precautions:"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not leave children unaccompanied"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not allow hotel staff to enter your room when you are alone – try to ensure a friend or relative is present"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid travelling in a taxi alone – if this is not possible, apply extra security by sharing the details of your driver, trip and live location with a friend"]},{"k":"li","s":["use reputable providers for any services and excursions, and carry out research beforehand"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid sharing personal contact or social media details"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Pickpocketing is rare in Egypt. Take the same precautions as you would in the UK."]},{"k":"p","s":["It might be helpful to make photocopies of your passport, visa and flight ticket to keep separately from originals when travelling. If your passport is lost or stolen, tell the police immediately and get a police report. You will need a police report to get visa stamps transferred to your replacement passport."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Scammers and touts at tourist sites"]},{"k":"p","s":["Be cautious in tourist areas, where scammers and touts sometimes target foreign nationals."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Egypt is predominantly an Islamic country. Take care to respect local traditions, customs, laws and religions. Seek to avoid offence, especially during Ramadan or when you visit religious areas."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Religion"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to encourage people to convert to Christianity or any other religion."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Ramadan"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ramadan is a holy month for Muslims. The dates vary by year and country and you should check this before you travel. During this time, it is culturally insensitive to eat, drink or smoke in public during daylight hours. It is advisable not to play loud music, dance or swear in public."]},{"k":"p","s":["Get more advice when you arrive from your tour guide, hotel or business contacts."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check opening hours of shops and restaurants"]},{"k":"li","s":["be aware that if hotels and restaurants are providing food or drink in fasting hours, they may separate non-fasting guests, for example with screens"]},{"k":"li","s":["follow local dress codes – clothing that does not meet local dress codes may cause more offence at this time"]},{"k":"li","s":["be aware that driving may be erratic, particularly when people are trying to get home at dusk"]},{"k":"li","s":["be patient and show tolerance"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police check ID regularly and will ask to see your passport. They may detain you if you cannot show your ID and your visa (or a digital/paper copy)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Dress code"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dress modestly, especially in rural areas, mosques and souqs (markets)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Behaviour in public"]},{"k":"p","s":["Couples showing physical affection in public is frowned upon. Holding hands is generally fine. Behaviour accepted in tourist resort areas may not be accepted elsewhere."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Alcohol laws"]},{"k":"p","s":["Drinking alcohol anywhere other than licensed restaurants, bars, or private homes or certain tourist resorts is illegal and can lead to arrest."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs penalties"]},{"k":"p","s":["Possession, use or trafficking of illegal drugs is a serious offence and can, even for small amounts, lead to life imprisonment or the death penalty. People who get a life sentence on drugs charges normally spend the rest of their lives in prison with no chance of parole or a pardon."]},{"k":"p","s":["Khat is illegal in Egypt."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Photography and filming restrictions"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must get a permit to enter Egypt with professional photography or film equipment. This includes photography umbrellas, artificial outdoor lighting gear and any equipment that occupies or blocks public roads."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to take or share photos that could be considered damaging to the country’s image."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Ministry of Tourism regulations","https://egymonuments.gov.eg/en/news/egyptian-government-decided-to-allow-egyptians-and-tourists-to-take-photographs-for-free/"]," on photography say that you must get an Egyptian citizen’s written permission before you take their photo. Taking pictures of children is prohibited. Do not photograph officials without their consent."]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to take photos of, or near, military property. This includes the Suez Canal."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are in any doubt, get permission before taking photos. There are sensitivities about taking photographs of:"]},{"k":"li","s":["embassies"]},{"k":"li","s":["government buildings and infrastructure"]},{"k":"li","s":["religious buildings"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Drones"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is illegal to use radio-controlled helicopters or drones to take photos near sensitive sites."]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not import, use or produce drones in Egypt unless the ",["Egyptian Ministry of Defence","https://www.mod.gov.eg/ModWebSite/Default.aspx"]," gives you permission beforehand. Anyone who uses or imports drones without permission could get a prison sentence of up to 7 years or a fine of 5,000 to 50,000 Egyptian pounds."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity is not explicitly criminalised in Egypt. However, the Egyptian authorities have prosecuted LGBT+ people under ‘debauchery’ laws, including people flying rainbow flags at public events."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is little public acceptance of same-sex relationships in Egypt. Same-sex couples showing affection in public are likely to receive negative attention."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be cautious about sharing content or having discussions of a sexual nature on social media, especially about sexual acts that are considered illegal. Some travellers have been arrested and imprisoned for this."]},{"k":"p","s":["You may be denied entry if your gender expression or physical appearance does not match the sex marker in your passport or your passport photograph."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Child custody law"]},{"k":"p","s":["Egyptian family law is very different from UK law, particularly around child custody. See ",["International parental child abduction","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/international-parental-child-abduction"]," for information and how to get assistance."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Insurance for adventure activities"]},{"k":"p","s":["Make sure your travel insurance, or the tour or dive company, will cover the costs for any air or sea rescue, which can cost more than 4,000 US dollars per hour. The Egyptian authorities will only start rescue operations when they are guaranteed payment. Book excursions for activities at your resort or through approved agents or tour operators."]},{"k":"p","s":["See Foreign travel insurance for more information."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Hot air balloons"]},{"k":"p","s":["Speak to your tour company before booking a hot air balloon flight. Some UK tour operators have stopped selling balloon flights because they have not been able to verify safety standards."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Watersports and cruises"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been recent incidents involving British nationals on diving boats/tours in the Red Sea. Some of these have included fatalities. Safety standards of Red Sea resort diving operators and boats vary considerably. Some operators may not provide adequate safety and insurance standards."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Marine Accident Investigation Branch (an independent investigative unit within the Department for Transport) published ",["guidance","https://www.gov.uk/government/news/safety-bulletin-12025-published"]," about liveaboard vessels on their website in February 2025. They recommend that prospective customers book liveaboard dive holidays only through reputable vendors who can provide assurance about the safety standards applicable to the dive boat. On arrival on board, customers should request that the crew provide a thorough safety briefing before departure."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should:"]},{"k":"li","s":["observe safety standards on board and exercise caution"]},{"k":"li","s":["make bookings through your tour representative or reputable operator if possible"]},{"k":"li","s":["not dive below your insurance policy’s depth limit – doing so will invalidate your cover"]},{"k":"p","s":["Shark attacks are rare, but there have been attacks on tourists in the Red Sea region. Monitor any updates from local authorities or your tour operator."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ⁠ ",["watersports safety advice","https://www.rospa.com/leisure-water-safety/water/advice/watersports-abroad"]," from the Royal Society for the Prevention of Accidents (ROSPA)."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Quad bikes and desert visits"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been several serious quad bike accidents involving British nationals in resort areas. Take the same precautions as you would in the UK. Safety standards vary considerably. Always wear a crash helmet."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Egypt, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You’ll need to have both the ",["1949 version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," and your UK driving licence with you in the car. You cannot buy an IDP outside the UK, so get one before you travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["You can drive in Egypt using your UK licence and an IDP for up to 6 months. If you plan to stay longer, you must apply for an Egyptian driving licence."]},{"k":"p","s":["Drivers and front seat passengers must wear seatbelts."]},{"k":"p","s":["Driving under the influence of alcohol or drugs is a serious offence in Egypt. If you are caught, you may get a fine and possible imprisonment."]},{"k":"p","s":["You may not be allowed to import a vehicle into Egypt. If you want to bring in a vehicle temporarily, you must get a ‘carnet de passage’ from the Automobile Association."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Accidents are common because of variable road conditions, dangerous driving and poor traffic law enforcement. Do not drive outside main cities and resorts at night. Make sure you have appropriate insurance."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Off-road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re travelling off road, use a qualified guide and get a permit from the ",["Ministry of Interior","https://emoves.moi.gov.eg/en/"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Rail and bus travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Serious bus crashes in recent years have caused many deaths, including tourists."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have also been fatal train collisions and derailments."]},{"k":"hs","s":["River and sea travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["Overcrowding and poor safety standards have caused accidents on Red Sea ferries and Nile cruises."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Egypt. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/egypt/warnings-and-insurance"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/egypt/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Egypt-Libya border"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to within 20km of the Egypt-Libya border, except for the town of El Salloum, where we advise against all but essential travel. There are active military operations targeting criminal activity in this area."]},{"k":"h","s":["North Sinai"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all travel to North Sinai Governate due to continuing criminal activity and terrorist attacks on police and security forces that have resulted in deaths. See ",["Terrorism in Egypt","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/egypt/safety-and-security#terrorism-in-egypt"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Northern part of South Sinai"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the northern part of South Sinai Governate, beyond the St Catherine-Nuweibaa road, except for the coastal areas along the west and east of the peninsula."]},{"k":"p","s":["We advise against all but essential travel due to criminal activity and terrorist attacks on police and security forces that have resulted in deaths. See ",["Terrorism","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/egypt/safety-and-security#terrorism"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan to travel in a 4-wheel-drive vehicle from mainland Egypt through the Suez crossing, you must get a permit from Travel Permits Department of the Egyptian Ministry of the Interior. You should book any activities outside of resort areas through approved agents or tour operators."]},{"k":"p","s":["Travel to Red Sea tourist areas in the southern part of the Sinai Peninsula is generally low risk. Security forces carry out routine checks on vehicles and at airports to protect resorts such as Sharm El Sheikh, Hurghada and Marsa Alam."]},{"k":"h","s":["Eastern part of Ismailiyah Governorate"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the parts of the Ismailiyah Governorate which are east of the Suez Canal, due to ongoing military operations against terrorists and criminals. Egyptian authorities at checkpoints may prevent onward travel to this region. See ",["Terrorism","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/egypt/safety-and-security#terrorism"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Western Desert"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the area west of the Nile Valley and Nile Delta regions due to risks from criminal activity and military operations, except for:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Luxor, Qina, Valley of the Kings, Aswan and Abu Simbel"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Faiyum Governorate"]},{"k":"li","s":["the coastal areas between the Nile Delta and Marsa Matruh"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Marsa Matruh-Siwa road"]},{"k":"li","s":["the oasis town of Siwa"]},{"k":"li","s":["the Giza Governorate north-east of the Bahariya Oasis"]},{"k":"li","s":["the White Desert and Black Desert"]},{"k":"li","s":["the oasis towns of Bahariya, Farafra, Dakhla (Mut) and Kharga"]},{"k":"li","s":["the following roads and the desert area between them and the Nile valley: the road between Giza and Farafra and within 50km either side of this road (but FCDO advises against all but essential travel on the road between Bahariya and Siwa)"]},{"k":"li","s":["the road between Farafra, Dakhla (Mut) and Kharga"]},{"k":"li","s":["the road between Kharga and Baris"]},{"k":"li","s":["the road between Baris and Luxor"]},{"k":"p","s":["Tourists who enter the Western Desert areas of Bahariya Oasis, Farafra, the White Desert and Black Desert must apply for permits in advance and use an official guide. There is limited phone reception and poor medical facilities. Monitor travel advice and follow guidance from official guides and local authorities."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel on the road between Al Bawiti and Siwa, including the desert areas near the start of the road in Bahariya. The road is closed and there are military checkpoints."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you travel to the south-west corner of Egypt near the border with Sudan or Libya, you will need a permit from the ",["Ministry of Interior","https://emoves.moi.gov.eg/en"],". Bandits and armed groups operate in these areas."]},{"k":"p","s":["Be extremely cautious in all border areas."]},{"k":"h","s":["Hala’ib Triangle and Bir Tawil Trapezoid"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the Hala’ib Triangle and Bir Tawil Trapezoid."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Hala’ib Triangle remains disputed territory between Egypt and Sudan. If you’re in the Hala’ib Triangle and need urgent UK government support, contact the ",["British Embassy in Cairo","https://www.gov.uk/world/organisations/british-embassy-cairo"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Bir Tawil Trapezoid is unclaimed by either Egypt or Sudan. If you’re in this area and need urgent UK government support, ",["contact FCDO","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/home"]," in the last country you travelled through."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 123 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance or medical assistance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Egypt","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/71/egypt#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Egypt","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/71/egypt#General_Information"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue"]},{"k":"li","s":["tick-borne diseases such as Crimean-Congo haemorrhagic fever"]},{"k":"p","s":["Altitude sickness is a risk in parts of Egypt. Read more about ",["altitude sickness on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/26/altitude-illness"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Some prescribed and over-the-counter medicines may be controlled substances in Egypt. You may need permission from Egypt’s Ministry of Health before you arrive. If you do not have prior permission or the required documentation, you will not be allowed to take the medication into Egypt and you may be prosecuted."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you travel with prescription medication, carry a letter from your GP stating the:"]},{"k":"li","s":["condition you’re being treated for"]},{"k":"li","s":["amount of medication you will be carrying"]},{"k":"li","s":["medication is for your personal use only"]},{"k":"p","s":["For further information, contact the ",["Egyptian Consulate General in the UK","https://egyptconsulate.co.uk/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Egypt"]},{"k":"p","s":["Some hotel doctors have overcharged for treatment and medicines. Pharmacies outside hotels will often supply medication at lower prices. FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Egypt","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/egypt-list-of-medical-facilities-practitioners"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"p","s":["Access to specialised treatment for psychiatric illness is limited and may not be available outside major cities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Egypt"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ambulance: 123"]},{"k":"p","s":["Fire: 180"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police: 122"]},{"k":"p","s":["Tourist police: 126"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Egypt"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find?country=Egypt&serviceType=funeralDirectors"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Egypt"]," in Egypt"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Egypt","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/egypt-bereavement-information-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested in Egypt","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/egypt-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you are ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/egypt/british-embassy-cairo"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/egypt/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Egypt","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-egypt"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":30993,"change":"Updated information about Regional tensions (see ‘Warnings and insurance’ page)"},"https://theconversation.com/andy-burnham-and-climate-change-where-does-the-prime-minister-really-stand-296200":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"d8393585a419bf51818e3d2e17d816c11806262509b1bcb5cd4f00313d1cb0f6","read":"https://theconversation.com/andy-burnham-and-climate-change-where-does-the-prime-minister-really-stand-296200","bytes":181459,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Andy Burnham and climate change: where does the prime minister really stand?","by":[{"n":"James Jackson","r":"Lecturer and Research Fellow, School of Social Sciences, University of Manchester"}],"pub":"2026-11-10T14:24:03Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Since becoming prime minister, Andy Burnham has set out a relatively ambitious political agenda. He has outlined plans for a national care service, a “Great British Grid” and suggested a rethink of the UK’s relationship with the European Union."]},{"k":"p","s":["But one big omission of Burnham’s agenda is ",["climate","https://theconversation.com/topics/climate-change-27"]," policy. Amid the proposals that put clear water between him and his predecessor Keir Starmer, Burnham’s climate agenda remains unknown."]},{"k":"p","s":["The prime minister has reiterated his intention to reach ",["net zero by 2050","https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/sep/17/andy-burnham-uk-must-fully-commit-to-reaching-net-zero-by-2050"]," (though this target is contentious due to whether it actually reflects climate science as well as the extent to which it depends upon as yet unavailable technology). He has also pledged to attend Cop31, the UN climate summit currently underway in Turkey, in person. However, ",["the budget","https://theconversation.com/the-government-has-delayed-the-toughest-spending-decisions-but-heres-what-we-do-know-after-the-budget-295491"]," – Burnham’s first real opportunity to set out his vision – raised concerns about whether his political rhetoric could translate into a coherent climate policy."]},{"k":"p","s":["The budget revealed that increased defence spending would come at the cost of ",["£3 billion of cuts","https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/nov/08/andy-burnham-uk-climate-crisis-rosebank-jackdaw"]," at the Department for Energy Security and Net Zero. Subsidies for heat pumps, boilers, the warm homes plan, hydrogen and carbon capture and storage were the most prominent policies to be culled. This not only limits the UK’s green industrial policy, but suggests that energy security is still not considered to be part of national security."]},{"k":"h","s":["Energy security and rising bills"]},{"k":"p","s":["For years, the UK has been misaligned with its own carbon budgets set under the ",["Climate Change Act 2008","https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2008/27/contents"],". After the Conservatives began to question the ",["objective of net zero","https://www.gov.uk/government/speeches/pm-speech-on-net-zero-20-september-2023"]," towards the ",["end of their term in office","https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13876988.2023.2242799"],", the election of the first Labour government in 14 years was expected to ",["mark a turning point","https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/1467-923x.70151"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Burnham’s climate position has become entwined (and somewhat undermined) by energy security concerns following the so-called ",["fossilflationary","https://theconversation.com/as-the-climate-breaks-down-costs-go-up-welcome-to-climateflation-289352"]," pressures caused by the the US and Israel’s conflict with Iran. This has fed into the licensing of the ",["Jackdaw gasfield","https://theconversation.com/jackdaw-approval-what-a-new-north-sea-gasfield-means-for-uk-energy-security-bills-and-tax-291418"]," 150 miles east of Aberdeen. This is part of Burnham’s self-described ",["“pragmatic”","https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/jul/30/burnham-promises-pragmatic-approach-to-north-sea-oil-and-gas"]," approach to new drilling in the North Sea."]},{"k":"p","s":["The initial justification was that the move would improve energy security by increasing the domestic supply of oil and gas. But critics say that increasing supply has ",["little to no effect","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cq4g13pplyzzo"]," on domestic bills, as prices are set by international markets. Revenues made from the project will be used to finance an ",["Energy Wealth Fund","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm4g1zrl2nrqo"]," to invest in green technologies elsewhere."]},{"k":"p","s":["Then there’s the cost of living. As winter approaches, an increasing number of households will have to choose between ",["heating and eating","https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cglj9685nj0o"],". In response, Chancellor John Healey’s budget ",["removed green levies","https://www.cityam.com/energy-bills-green-levies-scrapped/"]," from consumer bills. These levies fund environmental programmes such as renewable schemes, but the money will now be sourced from general taxation."]},{"k":"p","s":["This does reduce bills in the short term. But the shift, combined with other cuts, raises doubts over the long term."]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s argued that removing green levies from bills will reduce the relative cost of energy. This is a salient argument as the UK approaches a period of higher energy demand to heat the country’s homes. But critics say that reducing support for green energy infrastructure will delay the advent of abundant cheap energy."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political and economic tests"]},{"k":"p","s":["Labour now has two challenges. The first is the political bind the party finds itself in. ",["Its manifesto","https://labour.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/Labour-Party-manifesto-2024.pdf"]," does not contain anything resembling an ambitious climate policy. Although Burnham represents a change at the top, he has not won the right to depart from the platform upon which Starmer was elected."]},{"k":"p","s":["Whether this represents a constraint on Burnham’s political vision or just more of the same on a limited climate policy is up for debate."]},{"k":"p","s":["The second is an economic bind due to the UK’s tight fiscal position. This is despite taxation being at a ",["historically high level","https://www.resolutionfoundation.org/publications/thin-end-of-the-wedge/"]," – much of which has come from ",["“fiscal drag”","https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-9687/"]," as taxpayers are pulled into higher tax bands."]},{"k":"p","s":["The broader issues are that ten-year UK gilt yields – the rate the government pays on its debt – have risen to ",["5.13%","https://markets.ft.com/data/bonds/tearsheet/summary?s=UK10YG"],". The irony of the rise in gilt yields is that they have increased primarily because of “fossilflation” as supply-side shocks have fed into energy prices."]},{"k":"p","s":["As a result, the Bank of England has ",["raised interest rates","https://theconversation.com/interest-rates-are-making-uk-homes-less-affordable-even-as-wage-rises-increase-faster-than-house-prices-296075"]," for the first time in three years, to 4%. This will further increase borrowing costs and make life less affordable for UK households."]},{"k":"p","s":["And yet Labour’s propositions only put its climate policy into further doubt. It remains to be seen whether it will grant a licence for the Rosebank oil field, west of Shetland, which is larger than Jackdaw. Unless the government bends to political pressure, Burnham and energy secretary Miatta Fahnbulleh could make the same arguments about energy security again."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the meantime, Burnham has signalled his desire to raise tariffs on ",["Chinese green technology","https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2026/10/07/burnham-preparing-to-raise-chinese-ev-tariffs/"]," to align the UK more closely to the EU. This would be a bid to answer security worries, but tariffs on the cheapest and largest source of green technology places climate second to geopolitical concerns."]},{"k":"p","s":["Under Burnham, climate policy has, at best, been lost amid the prime minister’s broader political platform. At worst, he has concluded it is a price worth paying to achieve his other objectives."]}],"chars":5270},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ghana":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"5e81061c12e683dcd672ac71e3d93e76a2e9810614ee0a9a118b2f1405eba95e","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/ghana","bytes":66018,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Ghana travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-20T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-11-04T17:07:08+00:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["Your travel insurance could be invalidated if you travel against advice from the Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office (FCDO)."]},{"k":"h","s":["Areas where FCDO advises against all but essential travel"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Bawku Municipality"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the Bawku Municipal area in the Upper East region of Ghana."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out ",["why FCDO advises against travel to Bawku Municipality","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ghana/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ghana/email-signup"]," when this advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/travelaware/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Ghana’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Ghana set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Ghana High Commission in the UK","https://www.ghanahighcommissionuk.com/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Ghana, your passport must have an expiry date at least 6 months after the date you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a visa to visit Ghana."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["Complete the ",["online visa application form on the Ghana High Commission website","https://www.ghanahighcommissionuk.com/"]," and make the online payment."]},{"k":"p","s":["You need to upload your supporting documents (including a yellow fever certificate) and provide hard copies. Print out the form, add supporting documents and send them by post as directed on the website. The visa processing time is 10 to 15 days."]},{"k":"p","s":["To get a visa in around 3 days, check if you can use the express service available in person from the ",["Ghana Premium Application Centre in London","https://ghanahighcommissionuk.com/london-access-premium-centre"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Extending your visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["To extend your stay, take the ",["visa extension form","https://home.gis.gov.gh/gis-forms/"]," and photos in person to the Ghana Immigration Service (GIS) office on Independence Avenue in Accra."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Ghana, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination."]},{"k":"p","s":["For full details about health entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Ghana guide","https://www.travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/89/ghana#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Ebola Screening"]},{"k":"p","s":["Additional screening for Ebola (Bundibugyo Virus) Disease is in place at border entry points into Ghana. International travellers must complete an online ",["Ghana Health Declaration","https://ehds.ghs.gov.gh/"]," before travel. You may also be required to undergo thermal screening and a travel history check on arrival."]},{"k":"p","s":["The World Health Organisation (WHO) has ",["declared this a Public Health Emergency of International Concern","https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Registering with the authorities"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you live in Ghana, you must register with the National Identification Authority (NIA) of Ghana and get a ",["non-citizen Ghana card","https://fims.org.gh/"],". You must register all foreign nationals aged 6 years and above in your family who are either:"]},{"k":"li","s":["permanently resident in Ghana"]},{"k":"li","s":["resident in Ghana for at least 90 days in any calendar year"]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about ",["goods you can take into or out of Ghana","https://gra.gov.gh/customs/"],". You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Ghana"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must declare all hard currency, using the declaration form provided on arrival. You can bring in up to a maximum Ghanaian cedi equivalent of 10,000 US dollars."]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not carry more than 10,000 dollars in value in any foreign currency. The Ghanaian authorities can ",["confiscate the entire amount","https://www.accra-airport.com/en/customs_accra_airport.php"]," if you are carrying cash of this value or more."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationality"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ghana recognises dual nationality. Ghanaian-British dual nationals can register with the Interior Ministry in Ghana and get a dual nationality card. You can produce this card when entering Ghana to show that you do not need a visa."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"p","s":["This guide also has safety advice for ",["regions of Ghana","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ghana/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Ghana"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Ghana cannot be ruled out. Attacks could be indiscriminate, including in places visited by foreign nationals, such as:"]},{"k":"li","s":["northern border areas"]},{"k":"li","s":["busy public locations across the country, such as: beach resorts"]},{"k":"li","s":["hotels, cafes and restaurants"]},{"k":"li","s":["places of worship"]},{"k":"p","s":["Several terrorist groups operate in neighbouring Burkina Faso near to Ghana’s northern border, including Jama’a Nusrat ul-Islam wa al-Muslimin (JNIM) and Islamic State. JNIM regularly conducts attacks in southern Burkina Faso near to the Ghanaian border. There have also been JNIM attacks in the northern region of Togo, which borders Ghana to the east."]},{"k":"p","s":["Stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports, and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Demonstrations in the capital Accra are normally well policed and peaceful, but sometimes they occur at short notice and can cause disruption. Protests may result in restriction of movement. Follow local media for updates on planned routes."]},{"k":"p","s":["Disputes can occur between local tribes. This can lead to inter-ethnic violence and civil unrest. Seek local advice and take precautions in areas where political tensions are heightened. If you encounter any type of demonstration or disturbance leave the area quickly and do not attempt to watch or photograph any demonstration or disturbance. See ",["Regional risks","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ghana/regional-risks"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your belongings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Cases of robbery, burglary and serious assault have become more common since 2021, and in some cases involve the use of weapons. Foreign nationals have been attacked and robbed at gunpoint. Street crime like pickpocketing and bag-snatching is on the increase in Accra. To reduce your personal risk:"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoid carrying large sums of money or valuables"]},{"k":"li","s":["use a hotel safe to store passport and valuables whenever possible"]},{"k":"li","s":["be alert when withdrawing cash from ATMs, and where possible go by pre-arranged transport"]},{"k":"li","s":["be particularly careful after nightfall and avoid walking alone"]},{"k":"li","s":["take care of your belongings when arriving at and leaving hotels"]},{"k":"li","s":["keep a copy of your passport’s photo page and your visa or entry stamp in a separate place"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Safety at beaches"]},{"k":"p","s":["Theft is the main problem at beaches, but there have been isolated incidents of violent crime and sexual assault in areas popular with tourists. Do not visit beaches alone and take care on public beaches."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Safety at airports"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are reports of a small number of thefts of luggage and travel documents at Kotoka International Airport. Make sure your passport is always secure and do not leave your bags unattended. Be wary of offers of help except from uniformed porters or officials. All permanent staff at the airport wear an ID card showing their name and a photo. ID cards without a photo are not valid."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re being collected at the airport, confirm the identity of your driver by asking for ID. British nationals have been robbed by impostors who have approached them around the main arrivals area pretending to be their driver."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Accommodation security"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been cases of burglaries in areas used by the international community, including Airport Residential, Cantonments, Ridge and Kokrobite, with greater frequency over the Christmas period. Make sure doors and windows are locked and keep all valuables in a secure lockbox. Set the burglar alarm if your accommodation has one."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Vehicle crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks on vehicles are increasing, sometimes involving the use of weapons. A British national died in one such attack in August 2021. Take particular care when your car or taxi is stationary in a busy area. Keep windows up and doors locked."]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been isolated incidents of crime taking place in all types of taxis, including licensed taxis, minibuses (‘tro tros’) and app-based taxi services. Use licensed taxis and check driver ID and the vehicle’s condition before you travel. Some hotels will book transport to pick you up on arrival. Avoid travelling alone in taxis after dark."]},{"k":"p","s":["Local police have highlighted the risk to vehicles on specific roads in Accra:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Graphic Road"]},{"k":"li","s":["George Walker Bush Highway"]},{"k":"li","s":["Accra Mall Roundabout"]},{"k":"li","s":["Awudome Cemetery Road"]},{"k":"li","s":["Pokuase-Amasaman Road"]},{"k":"li","s":["Teshie-Nungua Road"]},{"k":"li","s":["Labadi beach area"]},{"k":"li","s":["GIMPA road and surrounding areas"]},{"k":"li","s":["Kokrobite beach area"]},{"k":"p","s":["For longer trips outside major cities:"]},{"k":"li","s":["travel during daylight hours"]},{"k":"li","s":["travel in a convoy of at least 2 vehicles"]},{"k":"li","s":["get advice from local police"]},{"k":"li","s":["do not stop your vehicle if people at the roadside try to flag you down"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Criminal kidnap"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been reports in the media of criminally motivated kidnapping in Accra, Takoradi and Kumasi, including the targeting of foreign nationals. Kidnaps can be for financial or political gain. If you’re kidnapped, the reason for your presence in Ghana is unlikely to serve as a protection or secure your safe release."]},{"k":"p","s":["The long-standing policy of the British government is not to make substantive concessions to hostage takers. The British government considers that paying ransoms and releasing prisoners increases the risk of further hostage-taking."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Scams"]},{"k":"p","s":["Scam artists target foreign visitors and residents. The scams come in many forms – romance and friendship, business ventures, work and employment opportunities – and can cause great financial loss. Warn your friends and family to be sceptical if they’re asked to transfer funds to you in Ghana. Tell them to contact you to check that you’ve made this request."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Bank card fraud"]},{"k":"p","s":["Keep your card in sight when paying bills and look closely when you use an ATM in case a skimming device has been attached to it."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ghana is a conservative and deeply religious country. Although attitudes are largely modern and progressive, you should show respect for traditional values and morals."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Dress code"]},{"k":"p","s":["Dress modestly in public. Shorts and t-shirts are acceptable for both males and females, but it is best not to wear anything too revealing."]},{"k":"p","s":["Wearing military clothing including camouflage is illegal."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["It is advisable to carry ID. A copy of your passport’s photo page is acceptable. Keep the original in a safe place such as a hotel safe."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["The possession of Illegal drugs, including cannabis, carry severe penalties."]},{"k":"p","s":["Bail is not normally granted."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should expect a long jail sentence and heavy fines for possessing, using or smuggling illegal drugs, including when transiting through the airport."]},{"k":"p","s":["Even possession of small amounts of cannabis can lead to a prison sentence of more than 5 years, usually after a long and expensive legal process."]},{"k":"p","s":["Airports in Ghana have excellent technology and security for detecting illegal items. This is also used to scan the baggage of transiting passengers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Class A drugs like cocaine are likely to be laced with other substances. Foreign visitors, including British nationals, have died after taking these drugs."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Using cameras in secure areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["Photography near sensitive sites like military installations or the airport is illegal. Ask permission if you want to take a photograph of a building where there are guards on duty. Beware of self-appointed officials trying to charge fees to let you take pictures of well-known sites of interest."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Some same-sex sexual acts could lead to a prison sentence of between 3 and 25 years. Although there are no records of this being enforced, there were arrests at a gathering of the LGBT+ community in 2021. Ghanaian society is intolerant of people of the same sex showing affection in public. Many Ghanaians do not accept that such relationships exist."]},{"k":"p","s":["The Human Sexual Rights and Family Values Bill is not law, but its passage through Ghana’s Parliament could increase the risk of violence and other threats (such as blackmail and intimidation) to the LGBT+ community and their allies. Rhetoric by some politicians, religious leaders, and local media can incite violence and psychological abuse of the LGBT+ community. FCDO are aware of reported attacks against LGBT+ individuals."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Family law in Ghana"]},{"k":"p","s":["Ghanaian family law is very different from UK law, particularly when child custody becomes an issue. Ghana is not a signatory to the Hague Convention. This means:"]},{"k":"li","s":["the custody process might not be as straightforward as in cases where both countries are signatories"]},{"k":"li","s":["you might need to initiate legal proceedings in Ghana for custody or to have your child returned to the UK"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Money"]},{"k":"p","s":["While ATMs are common in large towns, they may not be available in rural areas. Many hotels, guesthouses and shops accept credit cards except for Mastercard, which is not widely accepted. Check ahead that you can make payment, and have back-up funds."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your card issuer before you travel in case they put a block on your account."]},{"k":"h","s":["Outdoor activities and adventure tourism"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Swimming safety"]},{"k":"p","s":["Swimming is dangerous on the beaches along the southern coast of Ghana due to rip tides and undertows. There are occasional reports of deaths of both adults and children."]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["water safety on holiday","https://www.rlss.org.uk/safety-on-holiday"]," from the Royal Life Saving Society."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can drive in Ghana using the ",["1949 version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," or a Ghanaian driving licence. Carry your driving documents with you."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving standards"]},{"k":"p","s":["Roads can be in a poor condition, particularly in rural areas. Grass or leaves strewn in the road often mean there is an accident or other hazard ahead."]},{"k":"p","s":["Street lighting is poor or non-existent. Avoid travelling by road outside the main towns after dark, when the risk of serious road accidents is much greater, and there is a heightened risk of attacks on vehicles (see the crime section). If you choose to drive at night be aware of impromptu police checkpoints. Do not give cash to the police as this can be perceived as a bribe."]},{"k":"p","s":["Safety standards of taxi services in Ghana are low. Driver training and vehicle standards will vary from providers in the UK. Drivers of ‘tro-tros’ and popular app-based taxi services are not centrally licensed. There is no responsible body that is accountable for your safety."]},{"k":"p","s":["Outside major towns and cities it’s safer to use pre-booked vehicles from reputable taxi companies."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Extreme weather and natural disasters"]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out what you can do to prepare for and respond to ",["extreme weather and natural hazards","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/tropical-cyclones"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Flooding"]},{"k":"p","s":["Flooding is common in the Upper West, North East, Northern and Savannah regions during the rainy season, from March to November."]},{"k":"p","s":["Heavy rainfall can also cause flooding in other parts of Ghana, including urban areas such as Accra and its surroundings. Flooding can disrupt road travel, public transport, electricity and other services, and conditions can change quickly following periods of intense rainfall."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow the advice of local authorities, monitor local weather reports and expect difficulties when travelling to affected areas."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Earthquakes"]},{"k":"p","s":["Earthquakes and tremors are a risk in Ghana. The US Federal Emergency Management Agency website has advice about ",["what to do before, during and after an earthquake","https://www.ready.gov/earthquakes"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Regional risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["This section has safety advice for regions of Ghana. It only covers regions where FCDO has specific advice."]},{"k":"p","s":["You should also read ",["FCDO’s overall travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ghana"]," and ",["safety and security advice","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ghana/safety-and-security"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Civil unrest in specific local regions of Ghana can build up at short notice and may become violent, sometimes involving weapons. Avoid large crowds and political protests. Take local advice if you’re in an area with heightened political tension. Local police may impose curfews to contain the situation. Curfews usually run from 7pm to 5am but times can vary. The authorities may put out a press release which is usually posted on the ",["Ministry of the Interior’s website","https://www.mint.gov.gh/category/press-release/"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Bawku Municipality"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO advises against all but essential travel to the Bawku Municipal area in the Upper East region of Ghana. This is due to the number of violent incidents. The Ghanaian military have deployed additional military to the region to strengthen security."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you must travel to Bawku, you are strongly advised to contact the regional police headquarters in Bolgatanga town before travelling. Frequent curfews have been put in place in Bawku, with requirements and times changing. Security checks, including roadblocks, are often in effect and you should be prepared to show identification if asked. Be prepared for the security response to change without notice."]},{"k":"p","s":["Since October there have been a number of attacks related to the conflict in Bawku on the Nasia -Walewale-Bolgatanga road (Northeast) and the Bolgatanga- Zebilla – Bawku – Pulmankom road (Upper East). Many people are arranging police escorts when travelling along these roads. British citizens should take this into consideration including any precautionary measures before deciding to travel along these roads."]},{"k":"h","s":["Savannah Region"]},{"k":"p","s":["In August, tribal conflict broke out in the western part of Savannah Region, near the Cote d’Ivoire and Burkina Faso borders. This has resulted in a number of deaths and displacement of communities. Visitors travelling in that region should keep informed about the situation, seek local advice and take precautions."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical number"]},{"k":"p","s":["Call 999 or 112 and ask for an ambulance."]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Ghana","https://www.travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/89/ghana#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Ghana","https://www.travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/89/ghana"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["yellow fever"]},{"k":"li","s":["malaria"]},{"k":"li","s":["dengue"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Marburg virus"]},{"k":"p","s":["In September 2022, Ghana declared an end to the Marburg virus disease outbreak that was first reported in July 2022. See ",["more details about the outbreak","https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2022-DON409"]," and ",["more information on Marburg virus and similar diseases","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/68/viral-haemorrhagic-fever"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Ghana"]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical facilities are poor outside towns. For serious medical treatment, medical evacuation will be necessary. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Ghana","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ghana-list-of-medical-facilities"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Ghana"]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone:"]},{"k":"li","s":["999 or 112 (ambulance, fire, police, coast guard and mountain rescue)"]},{"k":"li","s":["192 (police)"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Ghana"]," and ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Ghana"]," in Ghana"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Ghana","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ghana-bereavement-information"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Ghana","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ghana-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contacting FCDO"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad"]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["how to get help from the UK government abroad","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/ghana/british-high-commission-accra"]," including in an emergency."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help in the UK"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/ghana/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Ghana","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-ghana"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":21657,"change":"Updated information about Ebola screening in Ghana (‘Entry requirements’ page)."}};
